INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_28_m_28_1_46',
  'A developer is building a new application that makes API calls to various AWS services. To ensure the application is resilient to transient failures and avoids overwhelming downstream services, which AWS SDK best practice should be implemented?',
  'mcq',
  '["Implement a circuit breaker pattern to immediately fail requests after a threshold.","Use client-side caching to reduce the number of API calls.","Configure retries with exponential backoff and jitter for all API calls.","Increase the API call timeout to allow more time for services to respond."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is the AWS SDK best practice for handling transient failures. Implementing retries with exponential backoff and jitter prevents retry storms and allows downstream services time to recover. Exponential backoff increases the wait time between retries, and jitter adds randomness to prevent synchronized retries. Option A (Implement a circuit breaker pattern) is a valid resilience pattern but is typically used in conjunction with retries, not as a replacement for basic retry logic. Option B (Use client-side caching) improves performance but doesn''t address transient API call failures. Option D (Increasing API call timeout) might hide the underlying issue and consume resources longer, rather than intelligently managing retries.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain28_6',
  NOW(),
  11,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_28_1',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_28_m_28_1_47',
  'Which AWS service is specifically designed for serverless compute, allowing developers to run code without provisioning or managing servers?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon EC2","AWS Lambda","Amazon ECS","AWS Elastic Beanstalk"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) AWS Lambda is the serverless compute service that allows developers to run code without provisioning or managing servers. It automatically scales and manages the underlying infrastructure. Option A (incorrect) Amazon EC2 provides virtual servers (instances) that require users to provision, manage, and scale them. Option C (incorrect) Amazon ECS (Elastic Container Service) is a container orchestration service that still requires managing underlying EC2 instances or using AWS Fargate, which is serverless for containers but not the core compute service for functions. Option D (incorrect) AWS Elastic Beanstalk is an orchestration service for deploying and scaling web applications and services, but it still provisions and manages EC2 instances or other compute resources.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain28_6',
  NOW(),
  12,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_28_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_28_m_28_1_48',
  'A developer needs to implement a retry mechanism for an application that calls an external API. To prevent overwhelming the API with repeated requests during transient failures, which best practice should be incorporated into the retry logic?',
  'mcq',
  '["Implement immediate retries with a fixed delay.","Implement exponential backoff with jitter.","Implement a circuit breaker pattern without retries.","Implement a fixed number of retries with no delay."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) implementing exponential backoff with jitter is a best practice for retry mechanisms. Exponential backoff gradually increases the wait time between retries, giving the downstream service more time to recover. Jitter (randomized delay) helps prevent all retrying clients from hitting the service simultaneously, which can cause a ''retry storm'' and exacerbate the problem. Option A (incorrect) immediate retries with a fixed delay can still overwhelm a struggling service if many clients retry at the same fixed interval. Option C (incorrect) a circuit breaker pattern is valuable for preventing calls to a failing service, but it''s typically used in conjunction with retries, not as a replacement, and doesn''t directly address the pacing of retries. Option D (incorrect) a fixed number of retries with no delay is highly detrimental, as it will immediately re-stress a failing service, likely causing more failures and amplifying the problem.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain28_6',
  NOW(),
  13,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_28_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_28_m_28_1_49',
  'A development team is deploying a new microservice using AWS Lambda. They observe that cold starts are impacting the performance of critical, latency-sensitive API endpoints. To mitigate this issue and ensure consistent low latency for these functions, which Lambda concurrency feature should they configure?',
  'mcq',
  '["Reserved concurrency","Provisioned concurrency","Burst limits","Unreserved account concurrency"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) Provisioned concurrency is specifically designed to address cold starts for latency-sensitive applications. It keeps a specified number of execution environments initialized and ready to respond immediately to invocations, eliminating cold start delays. Option A (incorrect) Reserved concurrency guarantees a maximum number of concurrent invocations for a function, preventing it from being throttled by other functions, but it does not prevent cold starts for the reserved instances. Option C (incorrect) Burst limits refer to the rate at which Lambda can scale up new execution environments, but they don''t prevent cold starts for those new environments. Option D (incorrect) Unreserved account concurrency is the pool of concurrency available to functions that do not have reserved concurrency configured, and it does not mitigate cold starts.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain28_6',
  NOW(),
  14,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_28_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_28_m_28_1_50',
  'A developer needs to create an API endpoint for a serverless application that allows clients to invoke an AWS Lambda function directly via HTTP. The API should handle request routing, authentication, and throttling. Which AWS service is designed to fulfill this requirement?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS App Runner","Amazon API Gateway","AWS Step Functions","Amazon EventBridge"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) Amazon API Gateway is the fully managed service that allows developers to create, publish, maintain, monitor, and secure APIs at any scale. It is perfectly suited for creating HTTP/HTTPS endpoints that can invoke AWS Lambda functions, handling request routing, authentication, and throttling. Option A (incorrect) AWS App Runner is a fully managed service for deploying containerized web applications and APIs, but it''s not primarily an API management service for existing Lambda functions. Option C (incorrect) AWS Step Functions is a serverless workflow service used to coordinate multiple AWS services into business-critical applications, not for creating direct HTTP API endpoints. Option D (incorrect) Amazon EventBridge is a serverless event bus that makes it easier to connect applications together by routing events from various sources to targets, but it''s not an HTTP API endpoint service.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain28_6',
  NOW(),
  15,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_28_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
