  'An Amazon EC2 instance needs to securely access objects in an Amazon S3 bucket. What is the most secure and operationally efficient way to grant this access without embedding credentials in the application?',
  'mcq',
  '["Hardcoding IAM access keys directly into the application code on the EC2 instance.","Creating an IAM user with programmatic access and storing its credentials on the EC2 instance.","Assigning an IAM role to the EC2 instance with a policy granting S3 access.","Configuring the S3 bucket policy to allow public read access to all objects."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is the most secure and operationally efficient method. By assigning an IAM role to the EC2 instance, the instance automatically receives temporary credentials, eliminating the need to store or rotate long-term credentials. Options A and B are anti-patterns as they involve hardcoding or storing long-term credentials on the instance, which is a security risk. Option D, public read access, is highly insecure and violates the principle of least privilege.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain29_6',
