INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_30_m_30_4_36',
  'A development team wants to implement a deployment strategy for their Amazon ECS application that minimizes downtime and provides a quick rollback mechanism in case of issues. Which AWS CodeDeploy deployment strategy best meets this requirement?',
  'mcq',
  '["All-at-once deployment","Rolling deployment","Blue/green deployment","In-place deployment"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Blue/green deployment) is the best strategy for minimizing downtime and enabling quick rollbacks for Amazon ECS applications. In a blue/green deployment, a new environment (green) is set up with the updated application version alongside the existing production environment (blue). Traffic is then shifted to the green environment, and if issues arise, traffic can be quickly rolled back to the blue environment. Option A (All-at-once deployment) causes downtime if the new version fails and is not recommended for production (anti-pattern ap-3.3-001). Option B (Rolling deployment) updates instances in batches, which can still lead to downtime or issues during the update process. Option D (In-place deployment) is not a standard CodeDeploy strategy for ECS and typically refers to updating existing instances directly, which can cause downtime.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain30_6',
  NOW(),
  42,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_30_4',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_30_m_30_4_37',
  'When configuring an AWS CodeBuild project, where should a developer define the build commands, test commands, and artifact packaging instructions?',
  'mcq',
  '["In the CodeBuild project settings in the AWS Management Console.","In a `buildspec.yml` file in the source code repository.","As environment variables in the CodeBuild project.","In an AWS CloudFormation template for the CodeBuild project."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (In a `buildspec.yml` file in the source code repository) is the correct location. AWS CodeBuild uses a `buildspec.yml` file, which is a collection of build commands and related settings, in YAML format, that CodeBuild uses to run a build. This file is typically included in the root of the source code repository. Option A (In the CodeBuild project settings in the AWS Management Console) allows for some basic configuration but not the detailed build steps. Option C (As environment variables in the CodeBuild project) are used for passing configuration values, not for defining build logic. Option D (In an AWS CloudFormation template for the CodeBuild project) defines the CodeBuild project itself, not the specific build steps.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain30_6',
  NOW(),
  43,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_30_4',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_30_m_30_4_38',
  'A developer needs to implement a canary deployment for an AWS Lambda function, gradually shifting a small percentage of traffic to a new version before fully rolling it out. Which AWS Lambda feature facilitates this deployment strategy?',
  'mcq',
  '["Lambda versions","Lambda layers","Lambda aliases with traffic shifting","Lambda environment variables"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Lambda aliases with traffic shifting) is the correct feature for canary deployments. AWS Lambda aliases allow you to point to different versions of a Lambda function and configure weighted traffic routing between them. This enables gradual traffic shifting, which is essential for canary deployments. Option A (Lambda versions) are immutable snapshots of your function code and configuration, but they don''t inherently manage traffic shifting. Option B (Lambda layers) are used to manage dependencies. Option D (Lambda environment variables) are used for configuration, not deployment strategies.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain30_6',
  NOW(),
  44,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_30_4',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_30_m_30_4_39',
  'A developer is setting up an AWS CodeBuild project that requires access to a database password during the build process. To prevent storing this sensitive information in plaintext, which AWS service should be used to manage and retrieve the secret securely?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS IAM Roles","AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store SecureString","AWS Key Management Service (KMS)","AWS Secrets Manager"]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Option D (AWS Secrets Manager) is the most appropriate service for managing and retrieving database passwords and other secrets securely in a CI/CD pipeline. Secrets Manager allows for automatic rotation of secrets and provides fine-grained access control. Option B (AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store SecureString) is also a valid option for storing secrets securely and referencing them in `buildspec.yml`, but Secrets Manager is specifically designed for managing database credentials and other application secrets with features like automatic rotation. Option A (AWS IAM Roles) provides permissions but doesn''t store the secret itself. Option C (AWS Key Management Service (KMS)) is used for encrypting data, including secrets, but it''s not the primary service for storing and retrieving the secrets themselves, rather it''s used by Secrets Manager and Parameter Store.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain30_6',
  NOW(),
  45,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_30_4',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_30_m_30_4_40',
  'Which of the following is the correct order of stages in a typical AWS CodePipeline release process?',
  'mcq',
  '["Source, Deploy, Build, Test","Source, Build, Test, Deploy","Build, Source, Test, Deploy","Test, Source, Build, Deploy"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (Source, Build, Test, Deploy) represents the correct and logical order of stages in a typical AWS CodePipeline release process. First, the source code is retrieved (Source). Then, the code is compiled and packaged (Build). After that, automated tests are run (Test). Finally, the application is deployed to its target environment (Deploy). The other options present an illogical or incorrect sequence of operations.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain30_6',
  NOW(),
  46,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_30_4',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_30_m_30_4_41',
  'A company is deploying a critical web application on Amazon ECS and needs to ensure zero downtime during updates. They also require the ability to quickly roll back to the previous version if the new deployment fails. Which CodeDeploy deployment strategy should be chosen?',
  'mcq',
  '["All-at-once","Rolling update","Blue/green","Linear"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Blue/green) is the ideal CodeDeploy strategy for achieving zero downtime and quick rollbacks for critical applications on Amazon ECS. Blue/green deployments involve provisioning a completely new environment with the updated application (green) and then shifting traffic from the old environment (blue) to the new one. If issues occur, traffic can be instantly reverted to the blue environment. Option A (All-at-once) causes downtime if the new version fails (anti-pattern ap-3.3-001). Option B (Rolling update) updates instances in batches, which can still introduce issues during the update and doesn''t provide an instant rollback to the previous stable environment. Option D (Linear) is a traffic shifting option for Lambda, not a general CodeDeploy strategy for ECS.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain30_6',
  NOW(),
  47,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_30_4',
  'medium'
);

