VALUES (
  'q_30_m_30_2_31',
  'A developer wants to test an AWS Lambda function that processes events from an Amazon SQS queue. To avoid deploying to AWS for every test iteration, which tool allows local invocation of the Lambda function with custom test events?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CLI","AWS SAM CLI","Amazon SQS API","AWS CloudWatch Logs"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (AWS SAM CLI) is the correct tool for locally invoking AWS Lambda functions with custom test events, simulating real-world scenarios without deploying to AWS. Option A (AWS CLI) is a general-purpose tool but doesn''t offer the specialized local testing capabilities of SAM CLI. Option C (Amazon SQS API) is for interacting with SQS, not for local Lambda invocation. Option D (AWS CloudWatch Logs) is for monitoring logs, not for local testing.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain30_6',
  NOW(),
  92,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_30_2',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_30_m_30_2_32',
  'A software development team is working on a serverless application and needs to perform quick, iterative testing of their AWS Lambda functions and Amazon API Gateway endpoints without deploying them to the cloud. Which approach is MOST suitable for this requirement?',
  'mcq',
  '["Deploying the application to a dedicated development AWS account for every test.","Using the AWS SAM CLI to test Lambda functions and API Gateway locally.","Manually invoking Lambda functions in the AWS Management Console.","Testing the application exclusively against production AWS resources."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (Using the AWS SAM CLI to test Lambda functions and API Gateway locally) is the most suitable approach. The AWS SAM CLI allows developers to invoke Lambda functions and API Gateway locally, significantly speeding up the development and testing cycle and reducing costs. Option A (Deploying the application to a dedicated development AWS account for every test) is slower and incurs costs. Option C (Manually invoking Lambda functions in the AWS Management Console) is inefficient for iterative testing. Option D (Testing the application exclusively against production AWS resources) is an anti-pattern that risks data corruption and incurs unnecessary costs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain30_6',
  NOW(),
  93,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_30_2',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_30_m_30_2_33',
  'A developer is building a new application that uses an Amazon DynamoDB table. During local development, they want to test their application''s interactions with DynamoDB without connecting to a live AWS environment. Which solution should they implement?',
  'mcq',
  '["Use a mock library to simulate DynamoDB responses in their code.","Set up a dedicated DynamoDB table in a development AWS account.","Run DynamoDB Local on their development machine.","Connect to the production DynamoDB table with restricted permissions."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Run DynamoDB Local on their development machine) is the most effective solution. DynamoDB Local provides a downloadable version of DynamoDB that runs on a local machine, allowing developers to test their application''s interactions with DynamoDB without incurring costs or needing an internet connection. Option A (Use a mock library to simulate DynamoDB responses in their code) is possible but DynamoDB Local offers a more realistic simulation. Option B (Set up a dedicated DynamoDB table in a development AWS account) incurs costs and still requires an internet connection. Option D (Connect to the production DynamoDB table with restricted permissions) is an anti-pattern for local development, risking accidental data modification and incurring costs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain30_6',
  NOW(),
  94,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_30_2',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_30_m_30_2_34',
  'A developer needs to test an AWS Lambda function locally before deploying it to the cloud. Which AWS CLI-based tool allows for local invocation and debugging of Lambda functions defined by AWS SAM templates?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CLI","AWS CDK CLI","AWS SAM CLI","AWS CloudFormation CLI"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (AWS SAM CLI) is the correct tool. The AWS SAM CLI provides a local development and testing environment for serverless applications, allowing developers to invoke Lambda functions locally, debug them, and even simulate API Gateway endpoints without deploying to AWS. Option A (AWS CLI) is a general-purpose command-line interface but doesn''t offer specific local Lambda testing capabilities. Option B (AWS CDK CLI) is for interacting with the AWS Cloud Development Kit. Option D (AWS CloudFormation CLI) is not a standard tool for local Lambda testing.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain30_6',
  NOW(),
  95,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_30_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_30_m_30_2_35',
  'A development team wants to perform integration testing for their serverless application. They need to simulate AWS service interactions (e.g., Amazon S3, Amazon SQS) in their local development environment without incurring AWS costs. Which open-source tool is commonly used for mocking AWS services locally?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CLI","AWS SDK","LocalStack","AWS X-Ray"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (LocalStack) is a popular open-source tool that provides a fully functional local AWS cloud stack. It allows developers to run their AWS applications entirely offline, simulating various AWS services like S3, SQS, Lambda, and DynamoDB, which is ideal for local integration testing without incurring costs. Option A (AWS CLI) is for interacting with actual AWS services. Option B (AWS SDK) is a library for interacting with AWS services. Option D (AWS X-Ray) is a service for analyzing and debugging distributed applications.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain30_6',
  NOW(),
  96,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_30_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_30_m_30_2_36',
  'When developing an application that uses Amazon DynamoDB, a developer wants to avoid connecting to a live AWS account for local testing to prevent data corruption and unnecessary costs. Which tool allows for local development with DynamoDB?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon RDS Local","DynamoDB Local","AWS DataSync","AWS Database Migration Service"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (DynamoDB Local) is the correct tool. DynamoDB Local is a downloadable version of Amazon DynamoDB that runs on your local machine, enabling developers to test their applications against a local DynamoDB instance without interacting with AWS services or incurring costs (anti-pattern ap-3.2-001). Option A (Amazon RDS Local) does not exist; RDS is a managed service. Option C (AWS DataSync) is a data transfer service. Option D (AWS Database Migration Service) is for migrating databases.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain30_6',
  NOW(),
  97,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_30_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_30_m_30_2_37',
  'A developer is creating a new version of an AWS Lambda function and wants to test it thoroughly in a staging environment before routing all production traffic to it. Which Lambda feature allows for creating separate logical endpoints for different versions of a function?',
  'mcq',
  '["Lambda layers","Lambda environment variables","Lambda aliases","Lambda concurrency settings"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Lambda aliases) is the correct feature. Lambda aliases are pointers to specific Lambda function versions. They allow you to create separate logical endpoints (e.g., `PROD`, `BETA`, `DEV`) for your function, making it easy to manage and test different versions, including traffic shifting for canary deployments. Option A (Lambda layers) are for managing dependencies. Option B (Lambda environment variables) are for configuration. Option D (Lambda concurrency settings) control the maximum number of concurrent executions.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain30_6',
  NOW(),
  98,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_30_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_30_m_30_2_38',
  'What is the primary purpose of using the AWS SAM CLI for local Lambda testing?',
  'mcq',
  '["To deploy Lambda functions to production environments.","To compile Lambda function code into a deployable package.","To invoke and debug Lambda functions locally without deploying to AWS.","To monitor Lambda function metrics and logs in real-time."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (To invoke and debug Lambda functions locally without deploying to AWS) is the primary purpose of the AWS SAM CLI for local testing. It provides commands like `sam local invoke` and `sam local start-api` that simulate the AWS Lambda and API Gateway environments on a developer''s machine, allowing for rapid iteration and debugging. Option A (To deploy Lambda functions to production environments) is done using `sam deploy`. Option B (To compile Lambda function code into a deployable package) is done using `sam build`. Option D (To monitor Lambda function metrics and logs in real-time) is handled by Amazon CloudWatch and AWS X-Ray.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain30_6',
  NOW(),
  99,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_30_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_30_m_30_2_39',
  'A company wants to ensure that their development team does not accidentally test new Lambda features against production AWS resources, which could lead to data corruption and unexpected costs. What is the recommended best practice for testing in development environments?',
  'mcq',
  '["Test Lambda functions exclusively against production AWS resources.","Use AWS SAM CLI for local testing and dedicated dev/test accounts for pre-production validation.","Disable all testing until the application is fully deployed to production.","Perform all testing manually in the AWS Management Console."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (Use AWS SAM CLI for local testing and dedicated dev/test accounts for pre-production validation) is the recommended best practice. Testing against production resources risks data corruption and incurs costs (anti-pattern ap-3.2-001). Using the SAM CLI for local testing allows developers to iterate quickly without affecting live environments. Dedicated dev/test accounts provide isolated environments for pre-production validation. Option A (Test Lambda functions exclusively against production AWS resources) is an anti-pattern. Option C (Disable all testing until the application is fully deployed to production) is a highly risky approach. Option D (Perform all testing manually in the AWS Management Console) is inefficient and prone to errors.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain30_6',
  NOW(),
  100,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_30_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_30_m_30_2_40',
  'Which AWS service can be used to automate the execution of unit, integration, and end-to-end tests as part of a CI/CD pipeline?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CodeCommit","AWS CodeDeploy","AWS CodeBuild","AWS CodePipeline"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (AWS CodeBuild) is the correct service. AWS CodeBuild is a fully managed continuous integration service that compiles source code, runs tests, and produces software packages that are ready to deploy. It is designed to execute arbitrary commands, including test scripts, within a build environment. Option A (AWS CodeCommit) is for source control. Option B (AWS CodeDeploy) is for deploying applications. Option D (AWS CodePipeline) orchestrates the entire CI/CD workflow but relies on CodeBuild for the actual build and test execution.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain30_6',
  NOW(),
  101,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_30_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_30_m_30_2_41',
  'A developer is working on an API Gateway-backed serverless application. They want to create separate deployment environments for development, staging, and production. Which API Gateway feature allows for managing these distinct environments?',
  'mcq',
  '["API Gateway models","API Gateway authorizers","API Gateway stages","API Gateway usage plans"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (API Gateway stages) is the correct feature. API Gateway stages are logical references to a specific state of your API. They allow you to manage different versions of your API (e.g., `dev`, `test`, `prod`) and configure them independently, including caching, throttling, and logging settings. Option A (API Gateway models) define the data structure of request and response bodies. Option B (API Gateway authorizers) control access to API methods. Option D (API Gateway usage plans) help manage client access and throttling.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain30_6',
  NOW(),
  102,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_30_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_30_m_30_2_42',
  'What is the primary benefit of using mocking frameworks like LocalStack or moto for local development of AWS applications?',
  'mcq',
  '["They provide a secure way to access production AWS resources from a local machine.","They allow developers to simulate AWS services locally, reducing costs and enabling offline development.","They automatically generate AWS CloudFormation templates for infrastructure deployment.","They integrate with on-premises data centers for hybrid cloud solutions."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (They allow developers to simulate AWS services locally, reducing costs and enabling offline development) is the primary benefit. Mocking frameworks like LocalStack and moto create local emulations of AWS services, enabling developers to test their applications without deploying to actual AWS infrastructure, thus saving costs and allowing development without an internet connection. Option A (They provide a secure way to access production AWS resources from a local machine) is incorrect; they simulate, not access, production resources. Option C (They automatically generate AWS CloudFormation templates for infrastructure deployment) is not their primary function. Option D (They integrate with on-premises data centers for hybrid cloud solutions) is unrelated to their core purpose.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain30_6',
  NOW(),
  103,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_30_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_30_m_30_2_43',
  'A developer needs to create a dedicated, isolated environment for testing a new feature of their serverless application. This environment should mimic the production setup but use separate data and resources. Which combination of AWS features would best achieve this isolation?',
  'mcq',
  '["Using the same Lambda function and API Gateway, but with different environment variables.","Creating new AWS Lambda versions and API Gateway stages for the new feature.","Testing directly in the production AWS account during off-peak hours.","Relying solely on local testing with the AWS SAM CLI."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (Creating new AWS Lambda versions and API Gateway stages for the new feature) is the best approach for creating a dedicated, isolated testing environment. Lambda versions allow you to maintain distinct code and configuration, while API Gateway stages provide separate endpoints and configurations for different environments (e.g., `dev`, `staging`). This ensures isolation from production. Option A (Using the same Lambda function and API Gateway, but with different environment variables) provides some configuration differentiation but doesn''t offer full isolation of the API endpoint or underlying Lambda code version. Option C (Testing directly in the production AWS account during off-peak hours) is an anti-pattern (ap-3.2-001) that risks data corruption and is not true isolation. Option D (Relying solely on local testing with the AWS SAM CLI) is good for unit testing but insufficient for integration or end-to-end testing in a cloud-like environment.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain30_6',
  NOW(),
  104,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_30_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_30_m_30_2_44',
  'A developer wants to test their AWS Lambda function locally using event data. Which AWS SAM CLI command allows them to invoke a Lambda function locally with a specified event payload?',
  'mcq',
  '["sam deploy","sam build","sam local invoke","sam validate"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (sam local invoke) is the correct command. The `sam local invoke` command allows developers to test their AWS Lambda functions locally by providing a sample event payload, simulating how the function would be triggered in the cloud. Option A (`sam deploy`) is used to deploy the application to AWS. Option B (`sam build`) compiles and packages the application. Option D (`sam validate`) checks the SAM template for syntax validity.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain30_6',
  NOW(),
  105,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_30_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_30_m_30_2_45',
  'A developer needs to manage different versions of an API Gateway API, such as `dev`, `staging`, and `prod`, each with its own configuration (e.g., caching, logging). Which API Gateway concept should be used?',
  'mcq',
  '["API Gateway resources","API Gateway methods","API Gateway stages","API Gateway models"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (API Gateway stages) is the correct concept. API Gateway stages are logical references to a specific state of your API. They allow you to manage multiple deployment environments (like `dev`, `staging`, `prod`) for a single API, each with its own configuration settings. Option A (API Gateway resources) represent the URL paths of your API. Option B (API Gateway methods) define the HTTP verbs (GET, POST, etc.) for a resource. Option D (API Gateway models) define the data structure of request and response bodies.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain30_6',
  NOW(),
  106,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_30_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_30_m_30_2_46',
  'What is an anti-pattern when testing AWS Lambda functions?',
  'mcq',
  '["Using AWS SAM CLI for local testing and debugging.","Testing Lambda functions exclusively against production AWS resources.","Utilizing dedicated development and test AWS accounts for pre-production validation.","Mocking AWS services with tools like LocalStack for integration tests."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (Testing Lambda functions exclusively against production AWS resources) is an anti-pattern (ap-3.2-001). This practice risks data corruption, incurs unnecessary costs, and can disrupt live services. Best practices involve using local testing tools like AWS SAM CLI and dedicated development/test environments. Option A (Using AWS SAM CLI for local testing and debugging) is a best practice. Option C (Utilizing dedicated development and test AWS accounts for pre-production validation) is a best practice for environment isolation. Option D (Mocking AWS services with tools like LocalStack for integration tests) is a best practice for local integration testing.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain30_6',
  NOW(),
  107,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_30_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_30_m_30_2_47',
  'Which AWS service can be integrated into a CI/CD pipeline to automatically execute unit tests, integration tests, and other custom build commands for an application?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CodeCommit","AWS CodeDeploy","AWS CodeBuild","AWS CloudFormation"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (AWS CodeBuild) is a fully managed continuous integration service that compiles source code, runs tests, and produces software packages that are ready to deploy. It is commonly used in CI/CD pipelines to automate the testing phase. Option A (AWS CodeCommit) is a source control service for hosting Git repositories. Option B (AWS CodeDeploy) automates software deployments to various compute services. Option D (AWS CloudFormation) is an infrastructure as code service for provisioning AWS resources.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain30_6',
  NOW(),
  108,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_30_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_30_m_30_2_48',
  'A software company is developing an application that interacts with several AWS services, including Amazon S3 and Amazon SQS. For local development and testing, they want to simulate these AWS services without deploying to the actual AWS Cloud. Which open-source tool can provide this functionality?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CLI","AWS SAM CLI","LocalStack","AWS Cloud Development Kit (AWS CDK)"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (LocalStack) is an open-source framework that provides a fully functional local cloud stack. It allows developers to run their AWS applications entirely on their local machine, simulating various AWS services like Amazon S3, Amazon SQS, AWS Lambda, and more. This is ideal for offline development and testing. Option A (AWS CLI) is a command-line interface for interacting with AWS services, not for local simulation. Option B (AWS SAM CLI) is for local testing of serverless applications, primarily Lambda and API Gateway, but not a broad range of AWS services like LocalStack. Option D (AWS Cloud Development Kit - AWS CDK) is a software development framework for defining cloud infrastructure in code, not for local service simulation.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain30_6',
  NOW(),
  109,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_30_2',
  'medium'
);

