INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_28_m_28_1_102',
  'A developer has created an API Gateway REST API and a resource. Order the steps to configure a GET method for this resource to integrate with an AWS Lambda function.',
  'ordering',
  '["Select the GET method for the resource","Choose the integration type as Lambda Function","Specify the Lambda function to integrate with","Save the method configuration"]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'To configure a GET method for an API Gateway resource, you first select the specific GET method you want to configure (Option A). Then, you choose the integration type, which in this case is Lambda Function (Option B). After that, you specify the particular AWS Lambda function that API Gateway should invoke (Option C). Finally, you save the method configuration (Option D) to apply the changes.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain28_6',
  NOW(),
  5,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_28_1',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_28_m_28_1_103',
  'An AWS Lambda function needs permissions to write logs to Amazon CloudWatch Logs and read from an Amazon S3 bucket. Order the steps to properly configure the IAM execution role for the Lambda function.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create a new IAM role","Specify the trust policy for the role to allow Lambda to assume it","Attach the necessary permissions policies (e.g., S3 read, CloudWatch Logs write)","Assign the configured IAM role to the Lambda function"]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'To properly configure an IAM execution role for a Lambda function, you first create a new IAM role (Option A). Then, you must specify the trust policy for this role, allowing the Lambda service to assume it (Option B). After that, you attach the specific permissions policies required for the function''s operations, such as S3 read and CloudWatch Logs write (Option C). Finally, you assign this fully configured IAM role to your Lambda function (Option D).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain28_6',
  NOW(),
  6,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_28_1',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_28_m_28_1_104',
  'A developer is building a resilient application that interacts with a downstream service and needs to implement a retry mechanism. Order the steps to implement exponential backoff with jitter for retries.',
  'ordering',
  '["Implement a basic retry loop for failed requests","Introduce a random jitter to the calculated delay","Calculate an exponential delay for each subsequent retry attempt","Define a maximum number of retry attempts or a maximum total delay"]'::json,
  '{0,2,1,3}',
  'To implement exponential backoff with jitter, you first need a basic retry mechanism for failed requests (Option A). Then, you calculate an exponential delay for each subsequent retry attempt, increasing the wait time after each failure (Option C). To prevent ''thundering herd'' issues, you introduce a random jitter to this calculated delay (Option B), spreading out retry attempts. Finally, you define a maximum number of retry attempts or a maximum total delay to prevent indefinite retries (Option D).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain28_6',
  NOW(),
  7,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_28_1',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_28_m_28_1_105',
  'A development team is building a new serverless application that processes user requests via an API Gateway and invokes an AWS Lambda function. The team wants to ensure the Lambda function is configured for optimal performance and cost-efficiency. Arrange the following steps in the recommended order for deploying and optimizing this Lambda function.',
  'ordering',
  '["Configure the AWS Lambda function''s memory and timeout settings based on observed performance tuning.","Develop and test the core logic of the AWS Lambda function locally.","Create an IAM execution role with necessary permissions for the Lambda function.","Deploy the AWS Lambda function code and associate it with the IAM execution role."]'::json,
  '{1,2,3,0}',
  'The correct order for deploying and optimizing an AWS Lambda function begins with developing the core logic (Option B). Next, an IAM execution role (Option C) must be created with the necessary permissions before the Lambda function can be deployed. After the function is deployed (Option D), performance tuning (Option A) should be conducted to determine the optimal memory and timeout settings for cost-efficiency and performance. Initializing the function with default settings and then tuning is a best practice.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain28_6',
  NOW(),
  8,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_28_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_28_m_28_1_106',
  'A developer is implementing a new microservice that uses the AWS SDK for Python to interact with Amazon DynamoDB. To ensure the application is resilient to transient failures and performs efficiently, they need to correctly implement retry logic and SDK client initialization. Order the following actions for best practice implementation.',
  'ordering',
  '["Initialize the AWS SDK DynamoDB client in the global scope of the application code.","Implement exponential backoff with jitter for all retry mechanisms when interacting with AWS services.","Define the specific AWS SDK operations (e.g., `put_item`, `get_item`) required for DynamoDB interaction.","Deploy the application code to its target AWS environment, such as AWS Lambda or Amazon ECS."]'::json,
  '{2,0,1,3}',
  'The process starts with defining the specific AWS SDK operations (Option C) that the application needs to perform. For efficiency, the AWS SDK client should be initialized in the global scope (Option A) to reuse connections across invocations, avoiding the anti-pattern of initializing clients inside a handler. Implementing exponential backoff with jitter (Option B) is crucial for resilience against transient failures and preventing retry storms. Finally, the application code is deployed (Option D) to its AWS environment.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain28_6',
  NOW(),
  9,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_28_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_28_m_28_1_107',
  'A company is migrating a legacy monolithic application to a serverless microservices architecture using Amazon API Gateway and AWS Lambda. They need to set up a new REST API endpoint to handle user authentication. Order the steps required to configure this API Gateway endpoint.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create a new Amazon API Gateway REST API.","Deploy the API to a stage (e.g., ''dev'', ''prod'').","Define a resource and an HTTP method (e.g., `/auth`, POST) for the API.","Integrate the HTTP method with an AWS Lambda function as the backend."]'::json,
  '{0,2,3,1}',
  'The first step is to create a new Amazon API Gateway REST API (Option A). Once the API exists, you define resources and HTTP methods (Option C) to represent the API''s endpoints. These methods are then integrated with a backend service, such as an AWS Lambda function (Option D). Finally, the API must be deployed to a stage (Option B) to make it accessible to users.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain28_6',
  NOW(),
  10,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_28_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_28_m_28_1_108',
  'A developer is tasked with updating an existing AWS Lambda function that processes images uploaded to Amazon S3. The update involves adding a new dependency that is shared across multiple Lambda functions. To manage dependencies efficiently and reduce deployment package size, the developer decides to use a Lambda layer. Order the steps to implement this solution.',
  'ordering',
  '["Update the AWS Lambda function to reference the newly created layer.","Package the shared dependency into a ZIP file.","Create a new AWS Lambda layer version from the ZIP file.","Test the updated AWS Lambda function with the layer."]'::json,
  '{1,2,0,3}',
  'The process begins by packaging the shared dependency into a ZIP file (Option B). This ZIP file is then used to create a new AWS Lambda layer version (Option C). Once the layer is available, the existing AWS Lambda function must be updated to reference this layer (Option A) so it can access the dependencies. Finally, the updated Lambda function should be thoroughly tested (Option D) to ensure it works correctly with the new layer.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain28_6',
  NOW(),
  11,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_28_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_28_m_28_1_109',
  'A company needs to implement a robust error handling strategy for an AWS Lambda function that processes messages from an Amazon SQS queue. To prevent ''poison-pill'' messages from blocking the queue and to enable investigation of failed messages, a Dead-Letter Queue (DLQ) must be configured. Order the steps to correctly set up this architecture.',
  'ordering',
  '["Configure the Amazon SQS queue''s Redrive Policy to send failed messages to the Dead-Letter Queue.","Create an AWS Lambda function to process messages from the primary SQS queue.","Create a Dead-Letter Queue (DLQ) using Amazon SQS.","Create the primary Amazon SQS queue that will trigger the Lambda function."]'::json,
  '{3,2,0,1}',
  'The first step is to create the primary Amazon SQS queue (Option D) that will serve as the event source. Next, a separate Dead-Letter Queue (DLQ) must be created (Option C) to capture messages that fail processing. After the DLQ is in place, the primary SQS queue''s Redrive Policy needs to be configured to direct failed messages to this DLQ (Option A). Finally, the AWS Lambda function (Option B) can be created and configured to process messages from the primary SQS queue, ensuring that failed messages are handled by the DLQ.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain28_6',
  NOW(),
  12,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_28_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_28_m_28_1_110',
  'A team is developing a new serverless application using AWS Lambda and Amazon API Gateway. They want to ensure that the Lambda function''s timeout is set appropriately to prevent excessive costs and resource consumption. Order the steps for determining and configuring an optimal timeout.',
  'ordering',
  '["Set the AWS Lambda function''s timeout to a value slightly higher than the observed average execution time.","Implement the core logic of the AWS Lambda function.","Deploy the AWS Lambda function and invoke it with various test scenarios.","Monitor the AWS Lambda function''s execution duration using Amazon CloudWatch logs and metrics."]'::json,
  '{1,2,3,0}',
  'The process begins with implementing the core logic of the AWS Lambda function (Option B). Once the function is developed, it needs to be deployed (Option C) and invoked with various test scenarios to simulate real-world usage. After invocations, the execution duration should be monitored using Amazon CloudWatch (Option D) to gather data on typical execution times. Finally, based on the observed data, the Lambda function''s timeout can be set (Option A) to a value that is slightly higher than the average execution time, preventing unnecessarily long execution and associated costs, which addresses the anti-pattern of setting timeouts too high.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain28_6',
  NOW(),
  13,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_28_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_28_m_28_1_111',
  'A developer is building an application that interacts with AWS services using the AWS SDK. To handle potential service interruptions gracefully and improve application resilience, they need to implement a robust retry mechanism. Order the following actions to correctly implement retries.',
  'ordering',
  '["Configure the AWS SDK client to use default retry behavior, which includes exponential backoff.","Identify the specific AWS SDK operations that might experience transient failures.","Test the application''s resilience by simulating transient failures in a development environment.","Customize retry settings with jitter if the default behavior is insufficient for specific use cases."]'::json,
  '{1,0,3,2}',
  'The first step is to identify the specific AWS SDK operations (Option B) that are prone to transient failures and require retry logic. Then, configure the AWS SDK client to use its default retry behavior (Option A), which typically includes exponential backoff. If the default settings are not sufficient for specific scenarios, customize retry settings with jitter (Option D) to further randomize retry attempts and avoid retry storms, addressing the anti-pattern of not implementing exponential backoff with jitter. Finally, test the application''s resilience (Option C) to ensure the retry mechanism works as expected.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain28_6',
  NOW(),
  14,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_28_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_28_m_28_1_112',
  'A development team is building a new microservice that processes incoming requests via Amazon API Gateway and invokes an AWS Lambda function. The Lambda function then calls a third-party API that is known to be occasionally unreliable, experiencing transient network issues or rate limiting. The team needs to ensure that calls to the third-party API are resilient and do not fail permanently due to temporary issues, while also avoiding overwhelming the third-party service. The solution must minimize latency for warm invocations and prevent retry storms.',
  'ordering',
  '["Implement an exponential backoff strategy with jitter for retries within the Lambda function code.","Deploy the updated AWS Lambda function code.","Initialize the third-party API client outside the Lambda handler function in the global scope.","Configure appropriate error handling logic to catch specific third-party API errors that warrant retries."]'::json,
  '{2,3,0,1}',
  'The most efficient approach starts by initializing the API client in the global scope (Option C) to reuse connections across warm invocations, reducing latency. Next, robust error handling (Option D) is crucial to identify which errors warrant retries. Implementing an exponential backoff with jitter (Option A) is the best practice for resilient retries, preventing retry storms and allowing the downstream service to recover. Finally, the updated code is deployed (Option B).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain28_6',
  NOW(),
  15,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_28_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_28_m_28_1_113',
  'A critical serverless application uses Amazon API Gateway to expose a REST API, which triggers an AWS Lambda function. During peak load, the Lambda function experiences throttling, leading to increased latency and failed requests for end-users. The development team needs to implement a solution to handle bursts of traffic more gracefully, ensuring that a certain baseline of concurrent invocations is always available, while also allowing for scaling beyond that baseline when needed. The solution should minimize cold starts for anticipated traffic spikes.',
  'ordering',
  '["Configure the AWS Lambda function with Provisioned Concurrency for a specified number of instances.","Monitor AWS Lambda metrics like `ConcurrentExecutions` and `Throttles` in Amazon CloudWatch.","Define a target utilization for the Provisioned Concurrency using Application Auto Scaling.","Set a Reserved Concurrency limit for the AWS Lambda function to prevent it from consuming all available account concurrency."]'::json,
  '{3,0,2,1}',
  'To manage concurrency, first, set a Reserved Concurrency limit (Option D) to guarantee a minimum capacity and prevent other functions from exhausting the account''s concurrency pool. Next, configure Provisioned Concurrency (Option A) to pre-initialize a specified number of execution environments, which significantly reduces cold starts for anticipated traffic. To dynamically adjust this, define a target utilization with Application Auto Scaling (Option C). Finally, continuous monitoring of `ConcurrentExecutions` and `Throttles` (Option B) in Amazon CloudWatch is essential to validate the configuration and identify further optimizations.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain28_6',
  NOW(),
  16,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_28_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_28_m_28_1_114',
  'A developer is tasked with deploying a new containerized microservice to AWS. The microservice needs to be highly available, scalable, and integrate seamlessly with other AWS services. The team wants to leverage a fully managed container orchestration service that abstracts away the underlying EC2 instances, allowing them to focus solely on container images and application code. The deployment process should be streamlined and repeatable.',
  'ordering',
  '["Define a task definition, specifying the container image, CPU, memory, and port mappings.","Create an Amazon ECS service definition, specifying the desired task definition, number of tasks, and network configuration.","Push the container image to Amazon Elastic Container Registry (Amazon ECR).","Create an Amazon ECS cluster with AWS Fargate as the capacity provider."]'::json,
  '{3,2,0,1}',
  'The first step is to create the Amazon ECS cluster (Option D), which provides the logical grouping for tasks. Next, the container image must be pushed to Amazon ECR (Option C) so it''s available for deployment. Then, a task definition (Option A) is created, which acts as a blueprint for running containers, referencing the ECR image. Finally, an Amazon ECS service (Option B) is created to maintain the desired number of tasks, run them on Fargate, and manage their lifecycle.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain28_6',
  NOW(),
  17,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_28_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_28_m_28_1_115',
  'A developer is building an event-driven application where an AWS Lambda function needs to process data from Amazon SQS. The Lambda function is designed to be idempotent, but there''s a concern about poison-pill messages or transient processing failures that could lead to messages being stuck in the queue or repeatedly failing. The team wants to ensure that failed messages are automatically isolated for later inspection without blocking the main queue.',
  'ordering',
  '["Configure the AWS Lambda function''s event source mapping to poll the main Amazon SQS queue.","Create a dead-letter queue (DLQ) in Amazon SQS to capture failed messages.","Configure the main Amazon SQS queue''s Redrive Policy to send messages to the DLQ after a certain number of receive attempts.","Create the main Amazon SQS queue that the Lambda function will process."]'::json,
  '{3,1,2,0}',
  'First, create the main Amazon SQS queue (Option D) that will receive messages. Then, create a separate dead-letter queue (DLQ) (Option B) which will serve as the destination for messages that fail processing. Next, configure the main queue''s Redrive Policy (Option C) to specify the DLQ and the `maxReceiveCount`, which determines how many times a message can be received before being moved to the DLQ. Finally, configure the AWS Lambda function''s event source mapping (Option A) to poll the main SQS queue, linking the function to its message source. This addresses the anti-pattern of not configuring a DLQ for SQS triggers.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain28_6',
  NOW(),
  18,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_28_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_28_m_28_1_116',
  'A developer is creating an AWS Lambda function that will be invoked by Amazon API Gateway. The function needs to access resources within a private Amazon VPC, such as an Amazon RDS database instance, without exposing the database to the public internet. The team wants to ensure secure and efficient communication between the Lambda function and the VPC resources.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create a Security Group for the AWS Lambda function to control outbound traffic to VPC resources.","Configure the AWS Lambda function to connect to the private subnets and the created Security Group within the VPC.","Ensure the private subnets have a NAT Gateway or VPC Endpoint configured if the Lambda function needs to access public internet resources (e.g., AWS services).","Create an Amazon VPC with private subnets where the target resources (e.g., Amazon RDS) reside."]'::json,
  '{3,0,1,2}',
  'The foundational step is to ensure an Amazon VPC with private subnets (Option D) is set up, as this is where the private resources reside. Next, a Security Group (Option A) is created for the Lambda function to define network access rules, allowing it to communicate with specific resources within the VPC. Then, the AWS Lambda function is configured to connect to these private subnets and associate with the Security Group (Option B), placing it within the VPC''s network. Finally, if the Lambda function also needs to initiate outbound connections to public internet resources (like other AWS services or external APIs), a NAT Gateway or VPC Endpoint (Option C) must be configured in the private subnets to allow this connectivity without exposing the function directly to the internet.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain28_6',
  NOW(),
  19,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_28_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_28_m_28_1_117',
  'A developer is optimizing an existing AWS Lambda function that processes images. The function''s deployment package is currently very large due to several machine learning libraries, leading to long cold start times and exceeding the standard deployment package size limit. The team wants to refactor the function to improve performance and manage dependencies more effectively.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create a Dockerfile that includes the application code and all large dependencies.","Push the container image to Amazon Elastic Container Registry (Amazon ECR).","Build the Docker image locally.","Create or update the AWS Lambda function, specifying the ECR image URI as the deployment package."]'::json,
  '{0,2,1,3}',
  'The process begins by creating a Dockerfile (Option A) that defines the container image, including the application code and all necessary large dependencies. Next, the Docker image is built locally (Option C) using the Dockerfile. This built image is then pushed to Amazon Elastic Container Registry (Amazon ECR) (Option B), making it available for AWS Lambda. Finally, the AWS Lambda function is created or updated (Option D) to use this ECR image URI as its deployment package, allowing it to leverage the larger container image size limit and pre-packaged dependencies. This directly addresses the anti-pattern of packaging large ML libraries in standard deployment packages.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain28_6',
  NOW(),
  20,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_28_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_28_m_28_1_118',
  'A development team is creating an application that uses Amazon API Gateway to expose a REST API. They need to implement custom authorization logic for specific API endpoints, where the authorization decision is made by an AWS Lambda function. The solution must provide fine-grained access control and integrate seamlessly with the API Gateway request flow.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create an AWS Lambda function that contains the custom authorization logic and returns an IAM policy.","Attach the created Lambda authorizer to the desired Amazon API Gateway method(s).","Deploy the Amazon API Gateway REST API to a stage.","Create a new Lambda authorizer in Amazon API Gateway, specifying the ARN of the Lambda function."]'::json,
  '{0,3,1,2}',
  'The first step is to create the AWS Lambda function (Option A) that will contain the custom authorization logic and return an IAM policy document. Next, a new Lambda authorizer is created within Amazon API Gateway (Option D), referencing the ARN of the authorization Lambda function. Then, this newly created Lambda authorizer is attached to the specific Amazon API Gateway method(s) (Option B) that require custom authorization. Finally, the Amazon API Gateway REST API must be deployed to a stage (Option C) for the changes to take effect and become accessible.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain28_6',
  NOW(),
  21,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_28_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_28_m_28_1_119',
  'A developer is working on a serverless application that processes large files uploaded to Amazon S3. The processing logic is implemented in an AWS Lambda function. To reduce the Lambda deployment package size and improve cold start times, the developer wants to use Lambda layers for common dependencies and shared utility code. The solution needs to ensure that the Lambda function can access these shared components efficiently.',
  'ordering',
  '["Configure the AWS Lambda function to use the newly created Lambda layer.","Create a .zip file containing the common dependencies and utility code.","Publish the .zip file as a new AWS Lambda layer version.","Update the AWS Lambda function code to import modules from the layer."]'::json,
  '{1,2,0,3}',
  'The process begins by creating a .zip file (Option B) that contains all the common dependencies and utility code intended for the layer. This .zip file is then published as a new AWS Lambda layer version (Option C), making the shared components available. Next, the AWS Lambda function is configured to use this newly created layer (Option A), linking it to the function''s execution environment. Finally, the AWS Lambda function''s code is updated (Option D) to import and utilize the modules and libraries provided by the layer, allowing the function to leverage the shared components. This approach helps reduce the deployment package size, addressing the anti-pattern of packaging large ML libraries in standard deployment packages.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain28_6',
  NOW(),
  22,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_28_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_28_m_28_1_120',
  'A developer needs to expose a serverless application through a public HTTP endpoint. Order the following steps to deploy a new Amazon API Gateway REST API.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create a new REST API in Amazon API Gateway.","Define resources and methods for the API.","Integrate methods with backend services like AWS Lambda.","Deploy the API to a stage (e.g., ''dev'', ''prod'')."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'The process of deploying an Amazon API Gateway REST API follows a logical sequence. Option A (Create a new REST API in Amazon API Gateway) is the initial step to establish the API container. Option B (Define resources and methods for the API) then specifies the API''s structure, such as paths and HTTP verbs. Option C (Integrate methods with backend services like AWS Lambda) connects these methods to the actual logic or data sources. Finally, Option D (Deploy the API to a stage (e.g., ''dev'', ''prod'')) makes the API accessible to consumers through a specific endpoint.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain28_6',
  NOW(),
  23,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_28_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_28_m_28_1_121',
  'A development team observes that their AWS Lambda functions are experiencing high cold start times and inconsistent performance. Order the following steps to optimize a Lambda function for improved performance and cost-efficiency.',
  'ordering',
  '["Analyze execution logs and metrics in Amazon CloudWatch to identify bottlenecks.","Adjust the Lambda function''s memory allocation.","Implement Provisioned Concurrency for critical functions.","Refactor code to initialize AWS SDK clients and database connections outside the handler."]'::json,
  '{0,3,1,2}',
  'Optimizing a Lambda function for performance and cost begins with understanding its current behavior. Option A (Analyze execution logs and metrics in Amazon CloudWatch to identify bottlenecks) is the crucial first step to gather data. Based on this analysis, Option D (Refactor code to initialize AWS SDK clients and database connections outside the handler) addresses common cold start issues by reusing resources across invocations. Option B (Adjust the Lambda function''s memory allocation) can then be tuned, as memory directly impacts CPU and network performance, and often reduces execution duration. Finally, Option C (Implement Provisioned Concurrency for critical functions) can be used for latency-sensitive applications to keep functions warm and minimize cold starts, but it should be considered after initial code and memory optimizations.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain28_6',
  NOW(),
  24,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_28_1',
  'medium'
);

