INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_30_m_30_1_54',
  'Which AWS services are used for managing source code repositories and container images for application deployment? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS CodeCommit","Amazon ECR","AWS CodeBuild","AWS CodePipeline","Amazon S3"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (AWS CodeCommit) is a fully managed source control service that hosts secure Git-based repositories for source code. Option B (Amazon ECR) is a fully managed Docker container registry for storing, managing, and deploying container images. Option C (AWS CodeBuild) is a build service. Option D (AWS CodePipeline) orchestrates CI/CD. Option E (Amazon S3) is for object storage and artifact management, not specifically source code repositories or container images.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain30_6',
  NOW(),
  58,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_30_1',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_30_m_30_1_55',
  'A developer needs to package a Python-based AWS Lambda function, including its dependencies, for deployment. The goal is to minimize the deployment package size to reduce cold start times. Which of the following approaches should the developer consider? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Use Lambda layers to manage common dependencies.","Include all development dependencies and test files in the deployment package.","Use a multi-stage Docker build to exclude development dependencies for containerized Lambda functions.","Package the entire virtual environment directly into the ZIP file.","Manually install dependencies on the Lambda execution environment at runtime."]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (correct) states to use Lambda layers to manage common dependencies. Lambda layers allow you to package libraries, a custom runtime, or other dependencies separately from your function code, reducing the size of the deployment package and enabling reuse across multiple functions. Option C (correct) states to use a multi-stage Docker build to exclude development dependencies for containerized Lambda functions. For containerized Lambda functions, a multi-stage Docker build allows you to use a larger base image for building (with development tools and dependencies) and then copy only the necessary runtime artifacts to a smaller, final image for deployment, significantly reducing the image size. Option B (incorrect) suggests including all development dependencies and test files in the deployment package. This is an anti-pattern (ap-3.1-001) as it significantly increases the deployment package size, leading to longer upload times and increased Lambda cold start times. Option D (incorrect) suggests packaging the entire virtual environment directly into the ZIP file. While this includes dependencies, it often includes unnecessary files (like pip caches, build tools, etc.) that increase package size. Using layers or multi-stage builds is more efficient. Option E (incorrect) suggests manually installing dependencies on the Lambda execution environment at runtime. This is generally not feasible or recommended for production Lambda functions as it would drastically increase cold start times and introduce reliability issues due to external network calls during invocation.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain30_6',
  NOW(),
  59,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_30_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_30_m_30_1_56',
  'A development team is preparing a new application for deployment to AWS. They need to manage their application''s source code using a fully managed Git repository and also require a service to store Docker images for their containerized components. Which AWS services are best suited for these requirements? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS CodeCommit for source code management.","Amazon ECR for Docker image storage.","AWS CodeArtifact for package management.","Amazon S3 for source code storage.","AWS CodeBuild for Docker image building."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) states AWS CodeCommit for source code management. AWS CodeCommit is a fully managed source control service that hosts secure Git-based repositories, making it ideal for managing application source code. Option B (correct) states Amazon ECR for Docker image storage. Amazon ECR (Elastic Container Registry) is a fully managed Docker container registry that makes it easy to store, manage, and deploy Docker container images. Option C (incorrect) suggests AWS CodeArtifact for package management. AWS CodeArtifact is a fully managed artifact repository service that makes it easy for organizations to securely store, publish, and share software packages, but it''s for application packages (e.g., npm, Maven, PyPI), not Git source code or Docker images. Option D (incorrect) suggests Amazon S3 for source code storage. While S3 can store any type of file, it is not a Git repository service and lacks version control features specific to source code management like branching and merging. AWS CodeCommit is the dedicated service. Option E (incorrect) suggests AWS CodeBuild for Docker image building. AWS CodeBuild can be used to build Docker images as part of a CI/CD pipeline, but it is not a service for storing Docker images; Amazon ECR is used for storage.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain30_6',
  NOW(),
  60,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_30_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_30_m_30_1_57',
  'When preparing a Docker container image for deployment to Amazon ECR, a developer wants to ensure the final image is as small as possible to optimize deployment times and reduce costs. Which practices should they adopt? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Use a multi-stage Docker build to separate build-time dependencies from runtime dependencies.","Include all development tools and test frameworks in the final production image.","Select a minimal base image (e.g., Alpine Linux) for the final stage of the build.","Store all application logs and temporary files directly within the container image.","Copy all source code, including documentation and unused assets, into the final image."]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (correct) states to use a multi-stage Docker build to separate build-time dependencies from runtime dependencies. Multi-stage builds allow you to use a larger image with build tools in an intermediate stage and then copy only the necessary runtime artifacts to a much smaller final image, significantly reducing the image size. Option C (correct) states to select a minimal base image (e.g., Alpine Linux) for the final stage of the build. Using a small, purpose-built base image for the final production stage of a Docker container helps to minimize the overall image size, which reduces pull times and storage costs. Option B (incorrect) suggests including all development tools and test frameworks in the final production image. This is an anti-pattern (ap-3.1-001) as it unnecessarily increases the image size, leading to longer pull times and increased storage costs. These tools are not needed at runtime. Option D (incorrect) suggests storing all application logs and temporary files directly within the container image. Logs and temporary files should be written to external volumes or sent to logging services (like Amazon CloudWatch Logs) rather than being stored within the image itself, as this would make the image larger and static. Option E (incorrect) suggests copying all source code, including documentation and unused assets, into the final image. Similar to including development dependencies, this practice adds unnecessary bulk to the container image. Only essential application code and assets should be included.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain30_6',
  NOW(),
  61,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_30_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_30_m_30_1_58',
  'A developer is packaging an AWS Lambda function that uses several external libraries. To optimize the deployment package size and reuse these libraries across multiple functions, which of the following strategies should be employed? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Utilize AWS Lambda layers for common dependencies.","Include only the necessary runtime dependencies in the deployment package.","Bundle all development dependencies and test files with the function code.","Manually install dependencies on the Lambda execution environment during cold starts.","Store all dependencies in an Amazon S3 bucket and download them at runtime."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) states to utilize AWS Lambda layers for common dependencies. Lambda layers allow you to package libraries, a custom runtime, or other dependencies separately from your function code, reducing the size of the deployment package and promoting reuse across multiple functions. Option B (correct) states to include only the necessary runtime dependencies in the deployment package. By carefully selecting and including only the libraries and modules that are essential for the function''s execution, the deployment package size can be minimized, which helps reduce cold start times and upload durations. Option C (incorrect) suggests bundling all development dependencies and test files with the function code. This is an anti-pattern (ap-3.1-001) as it unnecessarily increases the deployment package size, leading to longer upload times and increased Lambda cold start times. Development and test files are not needed in production. Option D (incorrect) suggests manually installing dependencies on the Lambda execution environment during cold starts. This approach is highly inefficient, significantly increases cold start times, and can introduce reliability issues due to external network calls during function invocation. Option E (incorrect) suggests storing all dependencies in an Amazon S3 bucket and downloading them at runtime. While technically possible, this adds complexity, increases cold start times due to download latency, and is less efficient and managed than using Lambda layers.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain30_6',
  NOW(),
  62,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_30_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_30_m_30_1_59',
  'A developer is using AWS CloudFormation to define the infrastructure for a new application. They need to manage application secrets securely and also store configuration parameters that might change between environments (development, staging, production). Which AWS services can they integrate with CloudFormation to handle these requirements? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Secrets Manager for sensitive data like API keys.","AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store for configuration parameters.","Amazon S3 for storing plaintext configuration files.","AWS CodeCommit for managing environment variables.","AWS Key Management Service (KMS) for direct secret storage."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) states AWS Secrets Manager for sensitive data like API keys. AWS Secrets Manager is designed for securely storing, managing, and retrieving sensitive information such as database credentials, API keys, and other secrets. CloudFormation can reference secrets stored in Secrets Manager. Option B (correct) states AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store for configuration parameters. Parameter Store allows for secure storage of configuration data and secrets as parameters. It supports both plain text and SecureString parameters, making it suitable for managing configuration parameters that vary between environments. CloudFormation can reference these parameters. Option C (incorrect) suggests Amazon S3 for storing plaintext configuration files. Storing plaintext configuration files, especially those containing sensitive information, in Amazon S3 is not a secure practice for managing secrets or dynamic parameters. While S3 can store files, it lacks the dedicated security features of Secrets Manager or Parameter Store for this use case. Option D (incorrect) suggests AWS CodeCommit for managing environment variables. AWS CodeCommit is a source control service for Git repositories and is not designed for managing runtime environment variables or configuration parameters. Option E (incorrect) suggests AWS Key Management Service (KMS) for direct secret storage. AWS KMS is a service for creating and managing cryptographic keys. While it''s used by Secrets Manager and Parameter Store for encryption, KMS itself does not directly store secrets or configuration parameters.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain30_6',
  NOW(),
  63,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_30_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_30_m_30_1_60',
  'A development team is using AWS Elastic Beanstalk to deploy their web application. They need to ensure that their application artifacts are packaged correctly for deployment and that the environment configuration can be managed as code. Which elements are crucial for this process? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["A source bundle containing the application code and dependencies.","Configuration files (.ebextensions) for environment customization.","A Dockerfile for defining the container image.","An AWS SAM template for serverless resource definition.","A CodeDeploy AppSpec file for deployment hooks."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) states a source bundle containing the application code and dependencies. For Elastic Beanstalk, the application is deployed as a source bundle (typically a ZIP or WAR file) that includes the application code, web server configuration files, and any necessary dependencies. Option B (correct) states configuration files (.ebextensions) for environment customization. Elastic Beanstalk uses .ebextensions configuration files to customize the environment, install software, and run scripts, allowing developers to manage environment configuration as code. Option C (incorrect) suggests a Dockerfile for defining the container image. While Elastic Beanstalk supports Docker environments, a Dockerfile is used to define a custom Docker image, not a universal requirement for all Elastic Beanstalk deployments (e.g., Java, Python, Node.js environments do not require a Dockerfile). Option D (incorrect) suggests an AWS SAM template for serverless resource definition. AWS SAM templates are used for defining serverless applications, not for configuring or deploying applications on Elastic Beanstalk. Option E (incorrect) suggests a CodeDeploy AppSpec file for deployment hooks. An AppSpec file is used by AWS CodeDeploy to manage deployments to EC2 instances, on-premises servers, or ECS, but it is not directly used for Elastic Beanstalk deployments which have their own deployment mechanisms.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain30_6',
  NOW(),
  64,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_30_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_30_m_30_1_61',
  'A developer is preparing a containerized application for deployment to Amazon ECS using AWS Elastic Beanstalk. They need to define the application''s container image and ensure that only the necessary runtime components are included to keep the image size small. Which of the following are key considerations? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Using a Dockerfile to define the container image.","Implementing multi-stage Docker builds to exclude development dependencies.","Including all build tools and test frameworks in the final container image.","Storing application logs directly within the container image layers.","Relying solely on Elastic Beanstalk''s default platform images without customization."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) states using a Dockerfile to define the container image. For containerized applications, a Dockerfile is essential to specify the base image, application code, dependencies, and runtime configuration for the container. Elastic Beanstalk uses this Dockerfile to build and deploy the container. Option B (correct) states implementing multi-stage Docker builds to exclude development dependencies. Multi-stage Docker builds are a best practice for minimizing container image size. They allow developers to use a larger image for building (with compilers, SDKs, etc.) and then copy only the essential runtime artifacts to a smaller, production-ready final image, excluding unnecessary development dependencies (Anti-pattern: ap-3.1-001). Option C (incorrect) suggests including all build tools and test frameworks in the final container image. This is an anti-pattern (ap-3.1-001) as it unnecessarily increases the image size, leading to longer pull times and increased storage costs. These tools are not needed at runtime. Option D (incorrect) suggests storing application logs directly within the container image layers. Application logs should be written to standard output/error (stdout/stderr) and collected by a logging solution (e.g., CloudWatch Logs) rather than being permanently stored within the container image, which would increase its size and make it static. Option E (incorrect) suggests relying solely on Elastic Beanstalk''s default platform images without customization. While default images are a starting point, for containerized applications, customization via a Dockerfile is often necessary to define the specific application environment and dependencies.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain30_6',
  NOW(),
  65,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_30_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_30_m_30_1_62',
  'A developer is preparing a containerized application for deployment to Amazon ECS. They need to package the application into a Docker image and store it in a private registry. Which AWS services are essential for this process? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Docker container packaging.","Amazon ECR for image storage.","AWS CodeCommit for Dockerfile storage.","AWS Lambda for container execution.","AWS CodeBuild for image building."]'::json,
  '{1,4}',
  'Option B (correct) states Amazon ECR for image storage. Amazon ECR (Elastic Container Registry) is a fully managed Docker container registry that makes it easy to store, manage, and deploy Docker container images. Option E (correct) states AWS CodeBuild for image building. AWS CodeBuild is a fully managed continuous integration service that can be configured to build Docker images from a Dockerfile as part of a CI/CD pipeline. Option A (incorrect) states Docker container packaging. While Docker container packaging is a process, it''s not an AWS service. The question asks for AWS services. Option C (incorrect) suggests AWS CodeCommit for Dockerfile storage. AWS CodeCommit is a Git repository service for source code, including Dockerfiles, but it''s not for storing the built Docker images themselves. Option D (incorrect) suggests AWS Lambda for container execution. While AWS Lambda now supports container images, Amazon ECS is the primary service for running containerized applications in this context, and Lambda is not involved in the packaging and storage of the image itself.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain30_6',
  NOW(),
  66,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_30_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_30_m_30_1_63',
  'A developer is preparing a new application for deployment to AWS Elastic Beanstalk. They need to define the application''s environment configuration as code and ensure that the application''s dependencies are correctly packaged. Which of the following are key elements for a successful deployment? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["A source bundle containing the application code and its dependencies.","Configuration files (.ebextensions) for environment customization.","An AWS SAM template for serverless resource definition.","A CodeDeploy AppSpec file for deployment lifecycle hooks.","A Dockerfile for defining the application''s build process."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) states a source bundle containing the application code and its dependencies. For Elastic Beanstalk, the application is deployed as a source bundle (typically a ZIP or WAR file) that includes the application code and any necessary dependencies. Option B (correct) states configuration files (.ebextensions) for environment customization. Elastic Beanstalk uses .ebextensions configuration files to customize the environment, install software, and run scripts, allowing developers to manage environment configuration as code. Option C (incorrect) suggests an AWS SAM template for serverless resource definition. AWS SAM templates are used for defining serverless applications, not for configuring or deploying applications on Elastic Beanstalk. Option D (incorrect) suggests a CodeDeploy AppSpec file for deployment lifecycle hooks. An AppSpec file is used by AWS CodeDeploy to manage deployments to EC2 instances, on-premises servers, or ECS, but it is not directly used for Elastic Beanstalk deployments. Option E (incorrect) suggests a Dockerfile for defining the application''s build process. While a Dockerfile defines a container image for Docker-based Elastic Beanstalk environments, it primarily defines the runtime environment, not the build process itself, and is not universally required for all Elastic Beanstalk platforms.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain30_6',
  NOW(),
  67,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_30_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_30_m_30_1_64',
  'Which of the following are common practices for preparing application artifacts for deployment to AWS Lambda to optimize performance and deployment size? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Using Lambda layers for shared dependencies.","Excluding development dependencies and test files from the deployment package.","Including all project files, including documentation and unused assets.","Packaging the entire virtual environment directly into the ZIP file.","Manually installing dependencies at runtime within the Lambda function."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) states using Lambda layers for shared dependencies. Lambda layers allow you to package libraries, a custom runtime, or other dependencies separately from your function code, reducing the size of the deployment package and enabling reuse across multiple functions. Option B (correct) states excluding development dependencies and test files from the deployment package. This is a best practice to minimize the deployment package size, which reduces upload times and Lambda cold start times (Anti-pattern: ap-3.1-001). Option C (incorrect) suggests including all project files, including documentation and unused assets. This unnecessarily increases the deployment package size, leading to longer upload times and increased Lambda cold start times. Only essential files should be included. Option D (incorrect) suggests packaging the entire virtual environment directly into the ZIP file. While this includes dependencies, it often includes unnecessary files (like pip caches, build tools, etc.) that increase package size. Using layers is a more optimized approach. Option E (incorrect) suggests manually installing dependencies at runtime within the Lambda function. This is highly inefficient, significantly increases cold start times, and can introduce reliability issues due to external network calls during function invocation.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain30_6',
  NOW(),
  68,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_30_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_30_m_30_1_65',
  'A development team is preparing a Dockerized microservice for deployment to Amazon ECS. The service has several development-only dependencies and large test files that are not required in the production environment. The team aims to optimize deployment times and reduce the attack surface for the production container images. Which two strategies should the developer implement to achieve these goals while adhering to best practices? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Implement a multi-stage Docker build process to separate build-time dependencies from the final runtime image.","Use .dockerignore to exclude development dependencies and test files from the build context sent to the Docker daemon.","Manually remove development dependencies and test files from the container image after the build completes but before pushing to Amazon ECR.","Store all dependencies, including development ones, in the container image and rely on runtime checks to exclude unnecessary files.","Utilize AWS CodeBuild to compress the entire Docker image before pushing it to Amazon ECR, reducing pull times."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is a best practice for Docker container packaging. Multi-stage builds allow developers to use a robust build environment with all necessary tools and dependencies in an intermediate stage, then copy only the essential runtime artifacts to a smaller, leaner final image. This significantly reduces the final image size, improving deployment times and reducing the attack surface. Option B (correct) is also a critical step. The .dockerignore file prevents unnecessary files (like development dependencies, test data, or source control metadata) from being sent to the Docker daemon during the build process, which speeds up the build context transfer and ensures these files are not accidentally included in any image layer. Option C (incorrect) is an anti-pattern ([ap-3.1-001]). Manually removing files after the build is inefficient, error-prone, and doesn''t prevent the files from being part of earlier image layers, which still contributes to image size and potential vulnerabilities. Option D (incorrect) directly contradicts the goal of reducing image size and attack surface. Including unnecessary dependencies increases the image size and potential security risks. Option E (incorrect) is not a standard or effective way to optimize Docker image size or deployment. While CodeBuild can be used for building, compressing the entire image before pushing is not how Docker image optimization works; the focus should be on the content of the image itself.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain30_6',
  NOW(),
  69,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_30_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_30_m_30_1_66',
  'A software company is developing a new serverless application using AWS Lambda and Amazon API Gateway. The development team needs to define the application''s resources, including Lambda functions, API endpoints, and DynamoDB tables, in a declarative way that supports both local development and deployment to AWS. The solution must enable easy management of infrastructure as code for the serverless components. Which two AWS services or frameworks are best suited for this requirement? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Serverless Application Model (AWS SAM).","AWS CloudFormation.","AWS Elastic Beanstalk.","AWS CodeCommit.","Amazon ECR."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is specifically designed for defining serverless applications. AWS SAM is an extension of AWS CloudFormation that provides a simplified way to define serverless resources like Lambda functions, API Gateway APIs, and DynamoDB tables. It supports local development and testing with the SAM CLI and translates into CloudFormation for deployment. Option B (correct) is the foundational service for infrastructure as code on AWS. AWS SAM templates are ultimately transformed into CloudFormation templates. Developers can directly use CloudFormation to define and manage all AWS resources, including serverless components, providing granular control and supporting complex deployments. Option C (incorrect) is a platform-as-a-service for deploying web applications, primarily for traditional server-based applications (e.g., EC2 instances). While it can host some serverless components, it''s not the primary or most efficient tool for defining and managing a purely serverless application''s infrastructure as code. Option D (incorrect) is a version control service for hosting Git repositories. While essential for storing application code and infrastructure templates, it does not define or deploy the application''s infrastructure itself. Option E (incorrect) is a fully managed Docker container registry. It''s used for storing and managing Docker images, which might be used by Lambda functions (container images) or ECS, but it does not define the application''s infrastructure as code.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain30_6',
  NOW(),
  70,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_30_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_30_m_30_1_67',
  'A development team is managing a complex application deployed on AWS Elastic Beanstalk. They need to automate the deployment process, including provisioning the underlying infrastructure, deploying the application code, and configuring environment-specific settings. The team wants to define the application''s resources and configurations in a version-controlled, declarative manner. Which two AWS services or frameworks are most appropriate for defining and managing this application''s infrastructure and deployment? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS CloudFormation.","AWS Elastic Beanstalk.","AWS SAM (Serverless Application Model).","AWS CodeDeploy.","Amazon ECR."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is the core AWS service for infrastructure as code. Developers can use CloudFormation templates to define the entire Elastic Beanstalk environment, including the application, environment configuration, and associated resources like databases or load balancers. This allows for version-controlled, repeatable, and automated provisioning of the infrastructure. Option B (correct) is the platform-as-a-service itself, which simplifies the deployment and management of applications. While Elastic Beanstalk handles much of the underlying infrastructure, developers still define the application version, environment configurations, and deployment policies within Elastic Beanstalk. It''s the target platform that also provides its own configuration mechanisms (e.g., .ebextensions) for customization. Option C (incorrect) is primarily designed for serverless applications (Lambda, API Gateway, DynamoDB) and is an extension of CloudFormation specifically for serverless resources. While Elastic Beanstalk can host some serverless components, SAM is not the direct or primary tool for defining and managing a full Elastic Beanstalk environment. Option D (incorrect) is a deployment service that automates code deployments to various compute services, including Elastic Beanstalk. While CodeDeploy can be integrated with Elastic Beanstalk for advanced deployment strategies, Elastic Beanstalk itself provides deployment capabilities, and CodeDeploy is not the primary service for *defining* the infrastructure or application configuration. Option E (incorrect) is a container registry. It''s used to store Docker images, which might be deployed to Elastic Beanstalk if it''s a Docker environment, but it does not define the application''s infrastructure or deployment logic.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain30_6',
  NOW(),
  71,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_30_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_30_m_30_1_68',
  'Which AWS services are primarily used for packaging and deploying containerized applications to a managed environment? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS SAM","Amazon ECR","AWS Elastic Beanstalk","AWS CloudFormation","AWS CodeCommit"]'::json,
  '{1,2}',
  'Amazon ECR (Option B) is a fully managed Docker container registry that makes it easy for developers to store, manage, and deploy Docker container images. AWS Elastic Beanstalk (Option C) is a platform as a service (PaaS) that supports deploying and scaling web applications and services developed with various languages, including Docker containers, handling the underlying infrastructure. Option A (AWS SAM) and Option D (AWS CloudFormation) are used for infrastructure as code, not directly for container packaging or as a managed container deployment platform. Option E (AWS CodeCommit) is a source control service.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain30_6',
  NOW(),
  72,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_30_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_30_m_30_1_69',
  'Which tools are most appropriate for defining and deploying serverless application infrastructure as code, including AWS Lambda functions and API Gateway configurations? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS CodeCommit","AWS SAM","AWS Elastic Beanstalk","AWS CloudFormation","Amazon ECR"]'::json,
  '{1,3}',
  'AWS SAM (Serverless Application Model) (Option B) is an open-source framework for building serverless applications, providing a simplified way to define serverless resources like AWS Lambda functions, APIs, and databases. AWS CloudFormation (Option D) is an infrastructure as code service that allows you to model and provision all your AWS resources needed for an application. AWS SAM templates are extensions of CloudFormation templates. Option A (AWS CodeCommit) is a version control service. Option C (AWS Elastic Beanstalk) is a platform for deploying web applications, not specifically for serverless infrastructure as code. Option E (Amazon ECR) is a container registry.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain30_6',
  NOW(),
  73,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_30_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_30_m_30_1_70',
  'When preparing an AWS Lambda function for deployment, which components are typically included in the deployment package to ensure the function runs correctly? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Application code","AWS SAM CLI","Required libraries/dependencies","Development test files","AWS CloudFormation templates"]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'The AWS Lambda deployment package must include the application code (Option A) that the Lambda function will execute. It also needs to contain all required libraries/dependencies (Option C) that the code relies on, which are not part of the Lambda runtime environment. Option B (AWS SAM CLI) is a local development tool, not part of the deployment package. Option D (Development test files) should be excluded from production deployment artifacts to reduce package size and improve cold start times, aligning with anti-pattern [ap-3.1-001]. Option E (AWS CloudFormation templates) define the infrastructure, not the function''s executable content.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain30_6',
  NOW(),
  74,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_30_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_30_m_30_1_71',
  'Which are key benefits of using AWS Elastic Beanstalk for deploying and managing web applications? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Serverless execution model without any underlying servers","Automated capacity provisioning and load balancing","Fine-grained control over individual EC2 instance patching","Integrated application health monitoring","Direct management of container orchestration for Amazon ECS"]'::json,
  '{1,3}',
  'AWS Elastic Beanstalk (Option B) automatically handles the deployment, capacity provisioning, load balancing, and auto-scaling of your application. It also provides integrated application health monitoring (Option D) through Amazon CloudWatch. Option A is incorrect because Elastic Beanstalk runs on underlying servers (e.g., EC2 instances), even if managed. Option C is incorrect as Elastic Beanstalk abstracts away much of the underlying infrastructure management, reducing fine-grained control for the developer. Option E is incorrect as Elastic Beanstalk manages its own environments and does not directly manage Amazon ECS orchestration.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain30_6',
  NOW(),
  75,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_30_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_30_m_30_1_72',
  'To optimize AWS Lambda function performance and reduce cold start times, which practices are recommended when packaging deployment artifacts? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Including all development dependencies in the package","Using multi-stage Docker builds for container images","Excluding unnecessary files like test suites and documentation","Packaging the AWS SAM CLI with the function code","Storing build secrets directly within the deployment package"]'::json,
  '{1,2}',
  'Using multi-stage Docker builds (Option B) is a best practice for containerized Lambda functions, allowing you to separate build-time dependencies from runtime dependencies, resulting in smaller final images. Excluding unnecessary files like test suites and documentation (Option C) significantly reduces the deployment package size, which directly contributes to faster cold start times and quicker deployment. Option A (Including all development dependencies) and Option D (Packaging test files) are anti-patterns [ap-3.1-001] as they increase package size. Option E (Storing build secrets directly) is a security anti-pattern.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain30_6',
  NOW(),
  76,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_30_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_30_m_30_1_73',
  'Which aspects can be configured for an application environment deployed using AWS Elastic Beanstalk? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Lambda alias traffic shifting rules","Environment variables for application settings","Amazon S3 bucket policies for static content","Auto Scaling group settings for instance capacity","AWS CodeCommit repository branch protection"]'::json,
  '{1,3}',
  'AWS Elastic Beanstalk allows you to configure environment variables (Option B) to pass settings to your application code. You can also customize the Auto Scaling group settings (Option D) to control how your application scales in response to demand. Option A (AWS Lambda alias traffic shifting rules) is specific to AWS Lambda. Option C (Amazon S3 bucket policies) is for Amazon S3, which might be used by the application but is not a direct configuration of the Elastic Beanstalk environment itself. Option E (AWS CodeCommit repository branch protection) is a feature of the source control service, not the deployment environment.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain30_6',
  NOW(),
  77,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_30_1',
  'medium'
);

