INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_2_35',
  'To enable distributed tracing for an AWS Lambda function, Amazon EC2 instance, or Amazon ECS service, which AWS component should a developer integrate into their application code?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS X-Ray SDK","CloudWatch Agent","AWS SDK for Python (Boto3)","AWS CLI"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (AWS X-Ray SDK) is the correct component to integrate into application code for enabling distributed tracing. It allows developers to instrument their code to send trace data to AWS X-Ray. Option B (CloudWatch Agent) is used to collect custom metrics and logs from EC2 instances and on-premises servers, not for distributed tracing. Option C (AWS SDK for Python (Boto3)) is a general SDK for interacting with AWS services, but the X-Ray SDK is specifically for tracing. Option D (AWS CLI) is a command-line interface for managing AWS services, not for in-application instrumentation.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  33,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_2',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_2_36',
  'A developer wants to track specific business-level metrics, such as the number of successful user sign-ups per minute, that are not provided by default AWS service metrics. Which AWS service should they use to publish these custom metrics?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS X-Ray","Amazon CloudWatch","Amazon S3","AWS IAM"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (Amazon CloudWatch) is the correct service for publishing custom metrics. Developers can define and publish their own metrics to CloudWatch, allowing them to monitor application-specific data. Option A (AWS X-Ray) is for distributed tracing. Option C (Amazon S3) is object storage. Option D (AWS IAM) is for identity and access management.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  34,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_2',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_2_37',
  'When an application generates a large volume of unique identifiers (e.g., user IDs) that need to be analyzed, but not as CloudWatch metric dimensions due to cost, which CloudWatch feature allows for efficient analysis of this high-cardinality data?',
  'mcq',
  '["CloudWatch Alarms","CloudWatch Dashboards","CloudWatch Embedded Metrics Format (EMF)","CloudWatch Synthetics"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (CloudWatch Embedded Metrics Format (EMF)) is the correct feature for handling high-cardinality data. EMF allows developers to embed custom metrics alongside detailed log events, enabling efficient analysis of high-cardinality data using CloudWatch Logs Insights without incurring high costs associated with traditional CloudWatch metric dimensions (ap-4.2-001). Option A (CloudWatch Alarms) are for triggering actions based on metric thresholds. Option B (CloudWatch Dashboards) are for visualizing metrics. Option D (CloudWatch Synthetics) is for monitoring endpoints and APIs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  35,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_2',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_2_38',
  'Which open-source library provided by AWS helps developers implement structured logging, tracing, and custom metrics more easily in their AWS Lambda functions?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS SDK for JavaScript","AWS CDK","AWS Lambda Powertools","AWS Amplify"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (AWS Lambda Powertools) is the correct open-source library that provides utilities for developers to implement structured logging, distributed tracing (with AWS X-Ray), and custom metrics (with CloudWatch Embedded Metrics Format) in their AWS Lambda functions with less boilerplate code. Option A (AWS SDK for JavaScript) is a general SDK for interacting with AWS services. Option B (AWS CDK) is a framework for defining cloud infrastructure in code. Option D (AWS Amplify) is a set of tools and services for building scalable mobile and web applications.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  36,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_2',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_2_39',
  'A developer wants to log detailed information about user requests, including unique session IDs, from their AWS Lambda function. Storing these unique IDs as CloudWatch metric dimensions would be prohibitively expensive. What is the recommended approach for analyzing this high-cardinality data?',
  'mcq',
  '["Use structured logs with CloudWatch Logs Insights.","Create a separate CloudWatch metric for each unique session ID.","Store session IDs in Amazon DynamoDB and query them directly.","Disable logging for session IDs to reduce costs."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (Use structured logs with CloudWatch Logs Insights) is the recommended approach. High-cardinality data like unique session IDs should be included in structured log events and then analyzed using CloudWatch Logs Insights, which is cost-effective for querying log data (ap-4.2-001). Option B (Create a separate CloudWatch metric for each unique session ID) is an anti-pattern (ap-4.2-001) as it would create an enormous number of distinct metrics, leading to very high costs. Option C (Store session IDs in Amazon DynamoDB) is a valid data storage solution but not the primary or most efficient way to analyze log-based high-cardinality data for observability. Option D (Disable logging for session IDs) would prevent necessary troubleshooting and analysis.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  37,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_2',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_2_40',
  'Which AWS service''s SDK must be integrated into an application running on an Amazon EC2 instance to send trace data for visualizing the application''s request flow and identifying performance bottlenecks?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS X-Ray SDK","AWS SDK for Java","Amazon CloudWatch Agent","AWS Systems Manager Agent"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option A (AWS X-Ray SDK) is the correct SDK to integrate into an application for sending trace data to AWS X-Ray. While there are language-specific AWS SDKs (like Option B, AWS SDK for Java), the X-Ray SDK is the specialized tool for distributed tracing. Option C (Amazon CloudWatch Agent) collects metrics and logs for CloudWatch. Option D (AWS Systems Manager Agent) is used for managing EC2 instances and on-premises systems.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  38,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_2',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_2_41',
  'A developer wants to emit custom metrics from their AWS Lambda function, ensuring that these metrics are associated with the relevant log events for easier debugging. Which CloudWatch feature facilitates this by embedding metrics within log data?',
  'mcq',
  '["CloudWatch Alarms","CloudWatch Dashboards","CloudWatch Embedded Metrics Format (EMF)","CloudWatch Logs Insights"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (CloudWatch Embedded Metrics Format (EMF)) is the correct feature. EMF allows developers to emit custom metrics as part of their structured log events, making it easier to correlate metrics with specific log data for enhanced observability and debugging. Option A (CloudWatch Alarms) are for triggering actions based on metric thresholds. Option B (CloudWatch Dashboards) are for visualizing metrics. Option D (CloudWatch Logs Insights) is for querying log data, but EMF is specifically for embedding metrics within those logs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  39,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_2',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_2_42',
  'Which AWS open-source library simplifies the implementation of common observability best practices, such as structured logging, tracing, and custom metrics, for serverless applications built with AWS Lambda?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Amplify","AWS CDK","AWS Lambda Powertools","AWS SAM CLI"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (AWS Lambda Powertools) is the correct open-source library. It provides a collection of utilities to help developers adopt best practices for observability in AWS Lambda functions, including structured logging, distributed tracing with AWS X-Ray, and custom metrics using CloudWatch Embedded Metrics Format. Option A (AWS Amplify) is a development platform for building web and mobile applications. Option B (AWS CDK) is for defining cloud infrastructure as code. Option D (AWS SAM CLI) is a command-line tool for building, testing, and deploying serverless applications.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  40,
