  '{2}',
  'Option C (AWS Lambda Powertools) is the correct open-source library. It provides a set of utilities to simplify the adoption of common observability best practices (structured logging, distributed tracing, custom metrics) for AWS Lambda functions. Option A (AWS SDK for Go) is a general SDK. Option B (AWS CLI) is a command-line interface. Option D (AWS Cloud Development Kit (CDK)) is for defining infrastructure as code.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  42,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_2',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_2_45',
  'A developer wants to add distributed tracing to an existing AWS Lambda function written in Node.js to gain insights into its performance and identify bottlenecks when interacting with other AWS services. Which tool or SDK should be integrated into the Lambda function code?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS SDK for JavaScript","AWS X-Ray SDK","Amazon CloudWatch Agent","AWS CLI"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the AWS X-Ray SDK. The X-Ray SDK allows developers to instrument their application code to send trace data to AWS X-Ray, enabling distributed tracing across various AWS services like Lambda, EC2, and ECS. This provides detailed insights into request flow and performance. Option A (AWS SDK for JavaScript) is used for interacting with AWS services programmatically, but it does not inherently provide distributed tracing capabilities. Option C (Amazon CloudWatch Agent) is used for collecting system-level metrics and logs from EC2 instances or on-premises servers, not for instrumenting Lambda functions for tracing. Option D (AWS CLI) is a command-line tool for managing AWS services, not for code instrumentation.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  43,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_2_46',
  'A development team needs to track specific business-level metrics, such as the number of successful order placements, within their AWS Lambda functions. These metrics are not available as standard CloudWatch metrics. Which approach should they use to capture these custom metrics?',
  'mcq',
  '["Publish events to Amazon SNS for each order.","Create custom metrics using the Amazon CloudWatch PutMetricData API.","Store order counts in an Amazon S3 bucket.","Log all order details to CloudWatch Logs and use Logs Insights."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is to create custom metrics using the Amazon CloudWatch PutMetricData API. This allows developers to define and publish their own application-specific metrics to CloudWatch, which can then be monitored, visualized on dashboards, and used to set alarms. Option A (Publish events to Amazon SNS) is for notifications, not for collecting time-series metric data. Option C (Store order counts in an Amazon S3 bucket) is for storage, not for real-time metric collection and monitoring. Option D (Log all order details to CloudWatch Logs and use Logs Insights) can be used for analysis, but it''s more suited for detailed event logging and querying, not for publishing aggregated, numerical time-series metrics efficiently.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  44,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_2_47',
  'What is the primary benefit of using structured logging in an application deployed on AWS Lambda?',
  'mcq',
  '["It reduces the storage cost of logs in Amazon CloudWatch Logs.","It automatically encrypts sensitive data within log messages.","It makes log data easier to parse, filter, and query programmatically or with tools like CloudWatch Logs Insights.","It prevents unauthorized access to log streams."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is the primary benefit of structured logging. By outputting logs in a consistent, machine-readable format (like JSON), it becomes significantly easier to parse, filter, search, and analyze log data using tools like CloudWatch Logs Insights, making troubleshooting and monitoring more efficient. Option A (reduces storage cost) is generally not true; structured logs might even be slightly larger than plain text. Option B (automatically encrypts sensitive data) is incorrect; structured logging is about format, not encryption. Option D (prevents unauthorized access) is incorrect; access control is handled by IAM policies, not by the logging format.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  45,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_2_48',
  'A developer needs to emit high-cardinality metrics (e.g., metrics with many unique dimension values like user IDs) from an AWS Lambda function to Amazon CloudWatch. To avoid incurring high costs associated with creating distinct metrics for each dimension combination, which AWS feature should be used?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS X-Ray annotations","Amazon CloudWatch Embedded Metrics Format (EMF)","Amazon CloudWatch custom alarms","AWS Lambda Powertools for metrics"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is Amazon CloudWatch Embedded Metrics Format (EMF). EMF allows developers to embed metric data within their structured log events, which are then automatically extracted and published as CloudWatch metrics. This approach is ideal for high-cardinality data, as it avoids the cost implications of creating millions of unique metric dimension combinations (as per anti-pattern ap-4.2-001). Option A (AWS X-Ray annotations) are for filtering traces, not for publishing metrics. Option C (Amazon CloudWatch custom alarms) are for reacting to existing metrics, not for defining how metrics are emitted. Option D (AWS Lambda Powertools for metrics) simplifies emitting metrics, but EMF is the underlying mechanism for handling high-cardinality scenarios efficiently.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  46,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_2_49',
  'Which component of AWS Lambda Powertools for Python helps developers implement structured logging, tracing, and custom metrics in their Lambda functions with minimal boilerplate code?',
  'mcq',
  '["Logger","Tracer","Metrics","All of the above"]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Option D (correct) is ''All of the above''. AWS Lambda Powertools for Python includes distinct utilities for Logger (structured logging), Tracer (distributed tracing with X-Ray), and Metrics (custom CloudWatch metrics, including Embedded Metrics Format). These components are designed to work together to simplify the implementation of observability best practices in Lambda functions. Each utility provides decorators and helper functions to reduce boilerplate code and ensure consistent instrumentation.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  47,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_2_50',
  'In AWS X-Ray, what are annotations primarily used for?',
  'mcq',
  '["To define the sampling rate for traces.","To add key-value pairs to traces for filtering and grouping in the X-Ray console.","To encrypt sensitive data within trace segments.","To configure alarms based on trace latency."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the primary use of annotations in AWS X-Ray. Annotations are simple key-value pairs that are indexed by X-Ray and can be used to filter traces in the X-Ray console or API. They are useful for adding business-specific metadata (e.g., customer ID, transaction type) to traces for easier debugging and analysis. Option A (sampling rate) is configured separately. Option C (encrypt sensitive data) is not the purpose of annotations; X-Ray encrypts data at rest by default. Option D (configure alarms) is done through CloudWatch Alarms, often based on X-Ray metrics, but annotations themselves don''t configure alarms.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  48,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_2_51',
  'Which statement best differentiates ''logging'', ''monitoring'', and ''observability'' in the context of cloud applications?',
  'mcq',
  '["Logging is about collecting raw data, monitoring is about setting alerts, and observability is about predicting future issues.","Logging is recording events, monitoring is observing system health with predefined metrics, and observability is the ability to understand internal states from external outputs.","Logging is for security audits, monitoring is for performance tuning, and observability is for cost optimization.","Logging is for developers, monitoring is for operations teams, and observability is for business analysts."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) provides the best differentiation. Logging involves recording discrete events and messages. Monitoring focuses on collecting and analyzing predefined metrics and logs to track the health and performance of a system against known thresholds. Observability, a broader concept, is the ability to infer the internal state of a system by examining its external outputs (logs, metrics, traces), allowing for deep exploration of unknown issues. The other options provide incomplete or incorrect distinctions.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  49,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_2_52',
  'A developer needs to add distributed tracing to an existing AWS Lambda function that processes incoming messages from Amazon SQS. The goal is to visualize the entire flow from SQS to Lambda and any downstream services. Which step should the developer take to enable X-Ray tracing for this Lambda function?',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure an Amazon CloudWatch alarm to trigger X-Ray tracing.","Enable active tracing in the Lambda function''s configuration and ensure the X-Ray SDK is used in the code.","Manually upload trace data to X-Ray using the AWS CLI after each invocation.","Modify the SQS queue policy to forward messages directly to X-Ray."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the correct step. To enable X-Ray tracing for an AWS Lambda function, you must first enable active tracing in the Lambda function''s configuration settings. Additionally, the Lambda function''s code should be instrumented using the AWS X-Ray SDK to capture detailed segments and subsegments for downstream calls. Option A (CloudWatch alarm) is for reacting to metrics, not enabling tracing. Option C (manual upload via CLI) is impractical and not how X-Ray is integrated with Lambda. Option D (modifying SQS policy) is incorrect; SQS integrates with Lambda, and Lambda then integrates with X-Ray.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  50,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_2_53',
  'A company wants to capture custom business metrics (e.g., ''items_added_to_cart'') from their AWS Lambda functions without incurring high costs if the metric values have many unique attributes (e.g., user IDs). Which AWS Lambda Powertools feature, leveraging a specific CloudWatch capability, should be used?',
  'mcq',
  '["Powertools Logger for structured logging.","Powertools Tracer for X-Ray segments.","Powertools Metrics with CloudWatch Embedded Metrics Format (EMF).","Powertools Event Handler for parsing events."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is Powertools Metrics with CloudWatch Embedded Metrics Format (EMF). AWS Lambda Powertools provides a Metrics utility that simplifies emitting custom metrics. When combined with EMF, it allows embedding metric data directly into CloudWatch Logs. CloudWatch then automatically extracts these metrics, making it highly cost-effective for high-cardinality data by avoiding the creation of numerous distinct metrics, which is an anti-pattern (ap-4.2-001). Option A (Powertools Logger) is for structured logging, not for emitting metrics. Option B (Powertools Tracer) is for distributed tracing with X-Ray. Option D (Powertools Event Handler) helps parse incoming events, not for metric emission.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  51,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_2_54',
  'A developer needs to instrument an AWS Lambda function to send custom metrics to Amazon CloudWatch. Which method should be used within the Lambda function code to publish these metrics?',
  'mcq',
  '["Using the AWS CLI to publish metrics.","Calling the PutMetricData API operation from the AWS SDK.","Writing metric data directly to CloudWatch Logs.","Configuring an Amazon SNS topic to forward metrics."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is calling the PutMetricData API operation from the AWS SDK. This is the programmatic way to publish custom metrics from within an application, such as an AWS Lambda function, to Amazon CloudWatch. Option A (using the AWS CLI) is for manual operations, not for code instrumentation. Option C (writing metric data directly to CloudWatch Logs) is not how metrics are published; while CloudWatch Embedded Metrics Format (EMF) uses logs, it''s an automated extraction, not direct metric publishing. Option D (configuring an Amazon SNS topic) is for notifications, not for publishing metrics.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  52,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_2_55',
  'A development team is using AWS Lambda Powertools for Python. They want to ensure that all log messages from their Lambda functions are consistently formatted as JSON, making them easier to parse and query. Which Powertools utility should they use?',
  'mcq',
  '["Tracer","Metrics","Logger","Event Handler"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is the Logger utility from AWS Lambda Powertools. The Logger utility provides structured logging capabilities, automatically formatting log messages as JSON and enriching them with contextual information, which greatly simplifies log analysis and querying in Amazon CloudWatch Logs Insights. Option A (Tracer) is for distributed tracing with AWS X-Ray. Option B (Metrics) is for emitting custom CloudWatch metrics. Option D (Event Handler) helps with parsing incoming event payloads.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  53,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_2_56',
  'A new e-commerce platform built with AWS Lambda and Amazon DynamoDB is experiencing intermittent issues where some user transactions are failing without clear error messages in the standard Lambda logs. The development team suspects that specific user interactions or data patterns might be causing these failures, possibly due to race conditions or unexpected data states. They need to instrument their code to capture detailed context, including user IDs and transaction IDs, for each invocation, and correlate this information across multiple services to identify the root cause. Which approach provides the MOST effective solution for this complex debugging scenario?',
  'mcq',
  '["Add custom log statements with user and transaction IDs to CloudWatch Logs, then use CloudWatch Logs Insights for querying.","Integrate AWS X-Ray SDK into the Lambda functions and add custom annotations for user and transaction IDs.","Create custom CloudWatch metrics with user and transaction IDs as high-cardinality dimensions.","Implement a centralized logging solution using Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose to send logs to a third-party analytics tool."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is to integrate AWS X-Ray SDK into the Lambda functions and add custom annotations for user and transaction IDs. X-Ray provides end-to-end distributed tracing, and custom annotations allow developers to attach specific key-value pairs (like user IDs, transaction IDs) to trace segments. This enables powerful filtering and searching within X-Ray, making it easy to find traces related to specific users or transactions and correlate them across services to identify the root cause of failures. Option A (incorrect) involves adding custom log statements to CloudWatch Logs. While this captures the data, correlating logs across multiple services and invocations using only CloudWatch Logs can be cumbersome and error-prone, especially in a distributed system (anti-pattern ap-4.1-001). Option C (incorrect) suggests creating custom CloudWatch metrics with user and transaction IDs as high-cardinality dimensions. This is a severe anti-pattern (ap-4.2-001) because each unique combination of dimensions creates a new metric, leading to enormous costs and potential service limits when dealing with high-cardinality data like user IDs. Option D (incorrect) is using Kinesis Data Firehose to send logs to a third-party tool. While a valid logging strategy, it introduces external dependencies and doesn''t inherently provide the distributed tracing capabilities or the direct correlation benefits of X-Ray within the AWS ecosystem.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  54,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_2_57',
  'A development team is building a serverless application using AWS Lambda and Amazon DynamoDB. They need to track the number of successful and failed DynamoDB operations performed by each Lambda function, along with the latency of these operations. This data will be used to create dashboards and trigger alarms. To ensure cost-effectiveness and avoid issues with metric cardinality, they want to capture this information efficiently. Which approach is the MOST appropriate for instrumenting the code?',
  'mcq',
  '["Use AWS X-Ray SDK to automatically capture DynamoDB subsegments and analyze trace data.","Implement custom CloudWatch metrics for each operation, using DynamoDB table names as dimensions.","Utilize CloudWatch Embedded Metrics Format (EMF) within Lambda to emit high-resolution metrics.","Add detailed log statements for each DynamoDB operation to CloudWatch Logs and use Logs Insights for aggregation."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is to utilize CloudWatch Embedded Metrics Format (EMF) within Lambda to emit high-resolution metrics. EMF allows developers to embed custom metrics within structured log events, which are then automatically extracted and published to CloudWatch Metrics. This is highly efficient for emitting multiple metrics from a single log event, supports high-resolution metrics, and is cost-effective as it leverages CloudWatch Logs for storage while providing metric capabilities. It''s ideal for capturing operational counts and latencies. Option A (incorrect) is using AWS X-Ray SDK. X-Ray is excellent for distributed tracing and visualizing request flows and latencies, but its primary purpose is not to emit aggregate metrics for dashboards and alarms. While it captures latency, it''s not the most direct or cost-effective way to get specific operational counts into CloudWatch Metrics. Option B (incorrect) is implementing custom CloudWatch metrics for each operation, using DynamoDB table names as dimensions. While table names are generally low-cardinality, if the team were to use high-cardinality data (e.g., item IDs) as dimensions, it would be an anti-pattern (ap-4.2-001) due to increased costs. EMF is generally a more efficient way to emit multiple metrics. Option D (incorrect) is adding detailed log statements to CloudWatch Logs. While logs contain the data, extracting metrics from logs for dashboards and alarms requires creating metric filters, which can be less efficient and more complex than using EMF for direct metric emission.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  55,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_2_58',
  'A developer is tasked with adding observability to an existing AWS Lambda function that processes customer orders. The function interacts with multiple downstream services. The requirement is to capture structured logs, custom business metrics (e.g., `order_processed_count`, `order_value`), and distributed traces with minimal code changes and adherence to AWS best practices. Which AWS toolset is purpose-built to simplify this instrumentation for Lambda functions?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS SDK for Python/Node.js with direct calls to CloudWatch Logs and CloudWatch Metrics APIs.","AWS Lambda Powertools for Python/TypeScript/Java.","Manual integration of AWS X-Ray SDK and custom loggers like `log4j` or `pino`.","Amazon CloudWatch Agent installed on a companion Amazon EC2 instance to collect logs and metrics."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is AWS Lambda Powertools. This open-source library provides a set of utilities for AWS Lambda functions that simplify common serverless best practices, including structured logging, custom metrics (using Embedded Metrics Format), and distributed tracing (integrating with AWS X-Ray SDK). It''s designed to reduce boilerplate code and adhere to best practices, directly meeting the requirements for minimal code changes and comprehensive observability. Option A (incorrect) is using the AWS SDK directly. While possible, it requires writing significant boilerplate code for structured logging, metric emission, and X-Ray integration, which goes against the ''minimal code changes'' requirement. Option C (incorrect) involves manual integration of X-Ray SDK and custom loggers. This is essentially what Lambda Powertools simplifies, making it a less efficient and more error-prone approach compared to using the Powertools. Option D (incorrect) is using the CloudWatch Agent on a companion EC2 instance. This is entirely inappropriate for a serverless Lambda function, as Lambda runs in a managed environment where developers do not have access to install agents on underlying EC2 instances.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  56,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_2_59',
  'A developer is working on an AWS Lambda function that processes real-time sensor data. To monitor the function''s performance and ensure data quality, they need to capture two types of information: (1) the total number of records processed per invocation, and (2) the average processing time per record. The `record_id` for each record is unique and highly variable. The team wants to use CloudWatch for monitoring, ensuring that the `record_id` is available for detailed log analysis but does not inflate metric costs. Which instrumentation strategy should the developer implement?',
  'mcq',
  '["Emit a custom CloudWatch metric for each `record_id` with its processing time.","Use AWS X-Ray to trace each record''s processing and add `record_id` as an annotation.","Implement structured logging to include `record_id` and processing time, and use CloudWatch Embedded Metrics Format for aggregate metrics.","Create a separate Amazon DynamoDB table to store `record_id` and processing time for later analysis."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is to implement structured logging to include `record_id` and processing time, and use CloudWatch Embedded Metrics Format (EMF) for aggregate metrics. Structured logging allows the high-cardinality `record_id` to be stored in CloudWatch Logs, where it can be queried using CloudWatch Logs Insights without incurring metric costs. EMF allows the developer to embed aggregate metrics (like total records processed and average processing time per invocation) within these structured log events, which are then automatically extracted and published to CloudWatch Metrics. This avoids the anti-pattern of using high-cardinality data as metric dimensions (ap-4.2-001) while still providing both detailed log analysis and aggregate metrics. Option A (incorrect) is to emit a custom CloudWatch metric for each `record_id`. This is a classic anti-pattern (ap-4.2-001). `record_id` is high-cardinality data, and using it as a metric dimension would create a new metric for every unique record, leading to extremely high costs and potential service limits. Option B (incorrect) is to use AWS X-Ray to trace each record''s processing. While X-Ray can trace individual requests and allow annotations, it''s primarily for distributed tracing and not for aggregate metric collection for dashboards and alarms. Tracing each individual record might also be overly verbose and costly depending on the volume. Option D (incorrect) is to create a separate DynamoDB table. This introduces additional operational overhead and cost for a solution that is primarily about observability, and it doesn''t integrate directly with CloudWatch for real-time monitoring and alerting.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  57,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_2_60',
  'A company is developing a new serverless application that processes sensitive financial transactions using AWS Lambda. They need to ensure that all application logs are structured, include correlation IDs for tracing, and contain specific business metadata (e.g., `transaction_type`, `customer_segment`). Additionally, they want to emit custom metrics for transaction success/failure rates. The solution must be easy to implement and maintain, following AWS best practices for observability. Which AWS service or library provides the MOST streamlined approach for this requirement?',
  'mcq',
  '["Manually configure a custom logger in the Lambda function to output JSON to CloudWatch Logs and use CloudWatch Metric Filters.","Integrate the AWS X-Ray SDK for tracing and implement custom CloudWatch metrics using the PutMetricData API.","Utilize AWS Lambda Powertools for structured logging, tracing, and metrics.","Send all Lambda function logs to an Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose stream for processing by a custom analytics application."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is to utilize AWS Lambda Powertools for structured logging, tracing, and metrics. Lambda Powertools is specifically designed to simplify the adoption of best practices for serverless applications. It provides decorators and utilities for structured logging (including correlation IDs and custom metadata), distributed tracing (integrating with X-Ray), and custom metrics (using CloudWatch Embedded Metrics Format). This approach streamlines implementation, reduces boilerplate code, and ensures adherence to AWS best practices. Option A (incorrect) involves manual configuration of JSON logging and CloudWatch Metric Filters. While functional, it requires more manual effort and doesn''t provide the integrated tracing and simplified metric emission that Powertools offers. Option B (incorrect) suggests integrating X-Ray SDK and using `PutMetricData`. This separates the concerns of logging, tracing, and metrics, requiring more code and potentially leading to inconsistencies. Powertools unifies these. Option D (incorrect) is sending logs to Kinesis Data Firehose for a custom analytics application. This introduces external complexity and doesn''t directly address the in-function instrumentation requirements for structured logging, tracing, and metrics within the AWS ecosystem.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  58,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_2_61',
  'A developer is instrumenting an AWS Lambda function that processes a high volume of events. They need to record a custom metric, `event_processing_time`, for each invocation. The function also generates detailed log messages that include a unique `event_id` for each processed event. The goal is to ensure that `event_id` is searchable in logs for debugging specific events, but `event_processing_time` is available as an aggregate metric in CloudWatch. The solution must be cost-effective and avoid creating excessive custom metrics. Which approach satisfies these requirements?',
  'mcq',
  '["Emit a custom CloudWatch metric for `event_processing_time` and include `event_id` as a dimension.","Use AWS X-Ray to trace each event and add `event_id` as an annotation to the trace segment.","Implement structured logging to include `event_id` and `event_processing_time`, and use CloudWatch Embedded Metrics Format for the metric.","Store `event_id` and `event_processing_time` in an Amazon DynamoDB table for later querying and analysis."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is to implement structured logging to include `event_id` and `event_processing_time`, and use CloudWatch Embedded Metrics Format (EMF) for the metric. This approach is highly efficient and cost-effective. The high-cardinality `event_id` is stored in CloudWatch Logs, where it can be easily searched and filtered using CloudWatch Logs Insights. The `event_processing_time` metric is embedded within the structured log, allowing CloudWatch to automatically extract and publish it as an aggregate metric without creating a new metric for each `event_id`. This directly addresses the anti-pattern (ap-4.2-001) of using high-cardinality data as metric dimensions. Option A (incorrect) is to emit a custom CloudWatch metric for `event_processing_time` and include `event_id` as a dimension. This is a classic anti-pattern (ap-4.2-001). Using `event_id` (high-cardinality data) as a metric dimension would create a new metric for every unique event, leading to extremely high costs and potential service limits. Option B (incorrect) is to use AWS X-Ray. While X-Ray is excellent for distributed tracing and `event_id` can be added as an annotation, its primary purpose is not to provide aggregate metrics for dashboards and alarms. It focuses on individual request traces. Option D (incorrect) is to store data in DynamoDB. This adds operational overhead, cost, and complexity for a task that is primarily about observability and metric collection, and it doesn''t integrate directly with CloudWatch for real-time monitoring and alerting.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  59,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_2_62',
  'A development team is building a new application using AWS Lambda functions. They want to implement a robust observability strategy that includes structured logging, custom business metrics, and distributed tracing. They also need to ensure that high-cardinality data, such as `customer_id` or `session_id`, is available for debugging and analysis without incurring excessive costs for CloudWatch metrics. Which approach BEST meets these requirements?',
  'mcq',
  '["Emit custom CloudWatch metrics for each `customer_id` and `session_id` as dimensions.","Use AWS X-Ray SDK for tracing and add `customer_id` and `session_id` as custom annotations.","Implement structured logging to include `customer_id` and `session_id` in CloudWatch Logs, and use CloudWatch Embedded Metrics Format for business metrics.","Send all application logs to a dedicated Amazon S3 bucket for archival and later analysis using Amazon Athena."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is to implement structured logging to include `customer_id` and `session_id` in CloudWatch Logs, and use CloudWatch Embedded Metrics Format (EMF) for business metrics. Structured logging allows high-cardinality data like `customer_id` and `session_id` to be stored in CloudWatch Logs, where it can be easily searched and filtered using CloudWatch Logs Insights without incurring metric costs. EMF allows embedding custom business metrics (e.g., transaction counts, processing times) within these structured log events, which are then automatically extracted and published to CloudWatch Metrics. This approach effectively avoids the anti-pattern (ap-4.2-001) of using high-cardinality data as metric dimensions while providing comprehensive observability. Option A (incorrect) is to emit custom CloudWatch metrics for each `customer_id` and `session_id` as dimensions. This is a severe anti-pattern (ap-4.2-001). Using high-cardinality data as metric dimensions would create a new metric for every unique customer or session, leading to enormous costs and potential service limits. Option B (incorrect) is to use AWS X-Ray SDK for tracing and add `customer_id` and `session_id` as custom annotations. While X-Ray is excellent for distributed tracing and annotations are searchable, its primary purpose is not to provide aggregate business metrics for dashboards and alarms. It focuses on individual request traces. Option D (incorrect) is sending logs to an S3 bucket for Athena analysis. While a valid archival and analytical strategy, it doesn''t provide real-time observability for structured logging, custom metrics, or distributed tracing within the CloudWatch ecosystem.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  60,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_2',
  'hard'
);

