INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_3_35',
  'To optimize the performance and cost of an AWS Lambda function, a developer should adjust its memory allocation. How does increasing the memory allocation typically affect a Lambda function''s performance?',
  'mcq',
  '["It decreases the available CPU power, making the function slower.","It increases the available CPU power, potentially making the function faster.","It only affects the amount of data the function can store in /tmp.","It has no impact on CPU power, only on memory-intensive tasks."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (It increases the available CPU power, potentially making the function faster) is the correct answer. In AWS Lambda, memory allocation is directly proportional to the CPU power allocated to the function. Increasing memory also increases the vCPU available, which can significantly improve the performance of CPU-bound functions. Option A is incorrect as it increases, not decreases, CPU power. Option C is partially true, but the primary performance impact is on CPU. Option D is incorrect because memory allocation directly influences CPU power.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  61,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_3',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_3_36',
  'Which AWS service provides an in-memory caching solution specifically designed to accelerate read performance for Amazon DynamoDB tables?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon CloudFront","Amazon DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX)","Amazon ElastiCache","API Gateway caching"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (Amazon DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX)) is the correct service. DAX is a fully managed, highly available, in-memory cache for DynamoDB that delivers up to a 10x performance improvement for read-heavy workloads. Option A (Amazon CloudFront) is a content delivery network. Option C (Amazon ElastiCache) provides managed Redis or Memcached, which can be used for general-purpose caching but is not specifically optimized for DynamoDB. Option D (API Gateway caching) is for caching API responses at the API Gateway level.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  62,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_3',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_3_37',
  'To process messages from an Amazon SQS queue using an AWS Lambda function in a cost-effective manner, especially when dealing with a high volume of messages, which SQS feature should be enabled for the Lambda trigger?',
  'mcq',
  '["Long polling","Visibility timeout","Batch processing","Dead-letter queue"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Batch processing) with AWS Lambda for Amazon SQS is the most cost-effective approach. Lambda can be configured to process multiple messages from an SQS queue in a single invocation, reducing the number of Lambda invocations and associated costs. Option A (Long polling) is an SQS feature for reducing empty responses, not directly related to Lambda cost optimization for processing. Option B (Visibility timeout) prevents other consumers from processing a message while it''s being handled. Option D (Dead-letter queue) is for handling messages that fail to be processed successfully.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  63,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_3',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_3_38',
  'An application uses Amazon API Gateway to expose a REST API that serves frequently accessed, static data. The development team wants to reduce the load on backend AWS Lambda functions and improve response times for clients. What is the most cost-effective optimization to implement?',
  'mcq',
  '["Enable API Gateway caching.","Increase the memory allocation for the backend Lambda functions.","Implement a custom caching layer using Amazon ElastiCache.","Migrate the static data to Amazon S3 and serve directly."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (Enable API Gateway caching) is the most cost-effective optimization for frequently accessed, static data. API Gateway caching stores responses from your backend, reducing the number of invocations to AWS Lambda and improving latency for subsequent requests (ap-4.3-001). Option B (Increase the memory allocation for the backend Lambda functions) might improve performance but does not reduce the number of invocations or the cost associated with them. Option C (Implement a custom caching layer using Amazon ElastiCache) is a valid caching strategy but requires more operational overhead compared to the built-in API Gateway caching for this specific scenario. Option D (Migrate the static data to Amazon S3 and serve directly) is a good practice for static content, but the question implies the API Gateway is already in use and the goal is to optimize its existing use.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  64,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_3',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_3_39',
  'Which AWS Lambda feature allows you to pre-initialize execution environments, reducing cold start latency for critical functions?',
  'mcq',
  '["Concurrency limits","Provisioned Concurrency","Memory tuning","Environment variables"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (Provisioned Concurrency) is the correct AWS Lambda feature for pre-initializing execution environments. It keeps functions initialized and ready to respond in milliseconds, significantly reducing cold start latency for critical applications. Option A (Concurrency limits) control the maximum number of simultaneous executions. Option C (Memory tuning) adjusts the memory and CPU allocated to the function. Option D (Environment variables) are used to pass configuration settings to the function code.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  65,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_3',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_3_40',
  'A web application frequently retrieves product images stored in Amazon S3. To improve loading times for end-users globally, which AWS caching service should be used to deliver content closer to the users?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon ElastiCache","Amazon DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX)","Amazon CloudFront","API Gateway caching"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Amazon CloudFront) is the correct service. CloudFront is a content delivery network (CDN) that caches content at edge locations worldwide, delivering it to users with lower latency. Option A (Amazon ElastiCache) is an in-memory caching service for databases. Option B (Amazon DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX)) is a cache for DynamoDB. Option D (API Gateway caching) is for caching API responses.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  66,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_3',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_3_41',
  'For a backend service that receives high volumes of requests and performs long-running computations, which processing approach is generally preferred to maintain responsiveness and prevent client timeouts?',
  'mcq',
  '["Synchronous processing","Blocking I/O operations","Asynchronous processing","Single-threaded execution"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Asynchronous processing) is generally preferred for high-volume, long-running computations. It allows the service to quickly acknowledge requests and process them in the background, preventing client timeouts and improving overall responsiveness. Option A (Synchronous processing) would force clients to wait for the long computation to complete. Option B (Blocking I/O operations) can lead to performance bottlenecks. Option D (Single-threaded execution) might be less efficient for concurrent long-running tasks.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  67,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_3',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_3_42',
  'A developer wants to implement a robust messaging system where an AWS Lambda function processes messages from a queue, ensuring that messages are not lost and can be retried if processing fails. Which AWS service should be used as the message queue?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon SQS","Amazon SNS","Amazon Kinesis Data Streams","Amazon EventBridge"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (Amazon SQS) is the correct AWS service for a robust message queue that integrates well with AWS Lambda for processing messages, offering features like message durability, retries, and dead-letter queues. Option B (Amazon SNS) is a pub/sub messaging service, primarily for fan-out scenarios, not a message queue for individual processing by Lambda. Option C (Amazon Kinesis Data Streams) is for real-time streaming data, which is a different use case. Option D (Amazon EventBridge) is a serverless event bus for connecting applications with data from various sources.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  68,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_3',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_3_43',
  'A developer has an AWS Lambda function that performs CPU-intensive image processing. To improve the function''s execution speed and reduce its duration, what is the most effective performance optimization they should consider?',
  'mcq',
  '["Increase the function''s timeout setting.","Increase the function''s memory allocation.","Reduce the number of concurrent executions.","Enable X-Ray tracing for the function."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (Increase the function''s memory allocation) is the most effective performance optimization for a CPU-intensive AWS Lambda function. In Lambda, increasing memory also proportionally increases the available CPU power, which directly speeds up CPU-bound tasks. Option A (Increase the function''s timeout setting) only prevents the function from timing out, it doesn''t improve execution speed. Option C (Reduce the number of concurrent executions) would limit scalability, not improve individual function performance. Option D (Enable X-Ray tracing) helps with observability and troubleshooting, but does not directly optimize the function''s execution speed.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  69,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_3',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_3_44',
  'For a highly dynamic web application that frequently accesses data from a relational database, which AWS service offers an in-memory caching solution to reduce database load and improve response times?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon CloudFront","Amazon DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX)","Amazon ElastiCache","API Gateway caching"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Amazon ElastiCache) is the correct service for providing an in-memory caching solution for relational databases. It supports Redis and Memcached, which are suitable for general-purpose caching to reduce database load. Option A (Amazon CloudFront) is a CDN. Option B (Amazon DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX)) is specifically for DynamoDB. Option D (API Gateway caching) is for caching API responses.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  70,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_3',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_3_45',
  'Which processing model allows an application to submit a request and immediately continue processing other tasks without waiting for a response, improving overall system throughput?',
  'mcq',
  '["Synchronous processing","Blocking I/O","Asynchronous processing","Transactional processing"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Asynchronous processing) is the correct processing model. It allows an application to submit a request and continue with other tasks without waiting for the request to complete, which significantly improves throughput and responsiveness. Option A (Synchronous processing) requires waiting for a response before proceeding. Option B (Blocking I/O) is a form of synchronous operation that can halt execution. Option D (Transactional processing) ensures atomicity and consistency, but doesn''t inherently define the waiting behavior.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  71,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_3',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_3_46',
  'A developer is building a serverless application where an AWS Lambda function needs to process a large number of messages from an Amazon SQS queue. To minimize costs and optimize resource utilization, how should the Lambda function be configured to interact with the SQS queue?',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure the Lambda trigger to process messages individually.","Increase the Lambda function''s memory to handle more messages per invocation.","Enable SQS batch processing for the Lambda trigger.","Set a very short visibility timeout on the SQS queue."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Enable SQS batch processing for the Lambda trigger) is the most effective way to minimize costs and optimize resource utilization when an AWS Lambda function processes a large number of messages from an Amazon SQS queue. This allows Lambda to process multiple messages in a single invocation, reducing the total number of invocations and associated costs. Option A (Configure the Lambda trigger to process messages individually) would increase invocations and costs. Option B (Increase the Lambda function''s memory) might help with processing larger batches but doesn''t inherently enable batching. Option D (Set a very short visibility timeout) could lead to messages being processed multiple times if the Lambda function fails or takes too long.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  72,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_3',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_3_47',
  'A developer needs to optimize an AWS Lambda function that is experiencing high latency due to frequent cold starts. Which specific Lambda feature can be configured to keep execution environments warm and ready for immediate invocation?',
  'mcq',
  '["Memory allocation","Provisioned Concurrency","Concurrency limits","Timeout setting"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (Provisioned Concurrency) is the specific AWS Lambda feature designed to address cold starts. It pre-initializes a specified number of execution environments, ensuring that invocations on these environments have very low latency. Option A (Memory allocation) affects CPU and memory for each invocation, but not cold starts. Option C (Concurrency limits) controls the maximum number of simultaneous executions. Option D (Timeout setting) defines the maximum execution duration.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  73,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_3',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_3_48',
  'Which AWS caching service is best suited for improving the performance of a global web application by distributing content, such as images and videos, from Amazon S3 buckets to users worldwide with low latency?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon ElastiCache","Amazon DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX)","Amazon CloudFront","API Gateway caching"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Amazon CloudFront) is the correct AWS service. CloudFront is a content delivery network (CDN) that caches content at edge locations around the world, reducing latency for global users accessing static and dynamic web content, including content from Amazon S3. Option A (Amazon ElastiCache) is an in-memory caching service for databases. Option B (Amazon DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX)) is a cache specifically for Amazon DynamoDB. Option D (API Gateway caching) is for caching responses from API Gateway endpoints.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  74,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_3',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_3_49',
  'An application needs to process user-uploaded files, which can take several minutes. To avoid blocking the user interface and ensure scalability, the processing should happen in the background. Which processing approach is most appropriate?',
  'mcq',
  '["Synchronous processing with increased timeout","Real-time processing with AWS Lambda","Asynchronous processing with a message queue","Direct file processing on the client-side"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Asynchronous processing with a message queue) is the most appropriate approach. This allows the application to quickly acknowledge the file upload, place a message in a queue (like Amazon SQS), and let a backend worker (like AWS Lambda or an EC2 instance) process the file independently, without blocking the user interface. Option A (Synchronous processing with increased timeout) would still block the UI and is not scalable. Option B (Real-time processing with AWS Lambda) might imply immediate processing, which is not suitable for ''several minutes'' tasks without an asynchronous pattern. Option D (Direct file processing on the client-side) is generally not feasible or secure for complex, long-running server-side tasks.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  75,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_3',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_3_50',
  'How does increasing the memory allocation for an AWS Lambda function typically impact its performance?',
  'mcq',
  '["It only increases the billing cost without affecting performance.","It primarily increases the function''s network bandwidth.","It also proportionally increases the available CPU, which can reduce execution duration.","It reduces the cold start time for the function."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is how increasing memory allocation impacts Lambda performance. When you increase the memory allocated to a Lambda function, AWS also proportionally increases the available CPU power. This often leads to faster execution times, especially for CPU-intensive tasks, thereby reducing the function''s duration and potentially its cost (as billing is based on duration * memory). Option A is incorrect because increased memory often leads to performance improvements. Option B is incorrect; while network performance can be influenced, the primary proportional increase is in CPU. Option D is incorrect; memory allocation does not directly reduce cold start time, though Provisioned Concurrency can help with that.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  76,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_3_51',
  'What is the primary purpose of enabling caching on an Amazon API Gateway endpoint?',
  'mcq',
  '["To encrypt data in transit between clients and the API Gateway.","To reduce the number of requests sent to the backend services and improve response times.","To authenticate and authorize API requests.","To automatically scale the backend services based on traffic."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the primary purpose of API Gateway caching. By caching responses from backend services, API Gateway can serve subsequent identical requests directly from the cache, significantly reducing the load on backend services (like AWS Lambda or EC2) and improving the response time for clients. Option A (encrypt data in transit) is handled by HTTPS/SSL. Option C (authenticate and authorize) is handled by API Gateway authorizers. Option D (automatically scale backend services) is a function of the backend services themselves (e.g., Lambda''s auto-scaling, EC2 Auto Scaling), not API Gateway caching.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  77,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_3_52',
  'A developer wants to distribute static assets (images, CSS, JavaScript) for a web application globally to users with low latency. Which AWS service is specifically designed for content delivery network (CDN) capabilities to achieve this?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon S3","Amazon CloudFront","Amazon Route 53","AWS Global Accelerator"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is Amazon CloudFront. CloudFront is a fast content delivery network (CDN) service that securely delivers data, videos, applications, and APIs to customers globally with low latency and high transfer speeds. It caches content at edge locations closer to users. Option A (Amazon S3) is object storage and can host static assets, but it doesn''t inherently provide global caching and low-latency delivery to users worldwide without a CDN. Option C (Amazon Route 53) is a highly available and scalable cloud Domain Name System (DNS) web service. Option D (AWS Global Accelerator) improves the availability and performance of applications by directing user traffic to optimal endpoints over the AWS global network, but it''s not a CDN for static content.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  78,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_3_53',
  'An application frequently reads data from an Amazon DynamoDB table, leading to high read latency and increased costs. The data is relatively static and accessed by many concurrent users. Which AWS service can be used to improve read performance and reduce the load on DynamoDB?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon RDS","Amazon Redshift","Amazon DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX)","Amazon ElastiCache"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is Amazon DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX). DAX is a fully managed, highly available, in-memory cache for DynamoDB that delivers up to a 10x performance improvement for read-heavy workloads. It is specifically designed to work with DynamoDB. Option A (Amazon RDS) is a relational database service, not a caching layer for DynamoDB. Option B (Amazon Redshift) is a data warehousing service, not a caching solution for operational databases. Option D (Amazon ElastiCache) provides in-memory caching for general-purpose use cases (like relational databases or application data) but is not specifically optimized as a transparent caching layer for DynamoDB like DAX.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  79,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_3_54',
  'A developer needs to implement an in-memory caching solution for a web application to reduce database load and improve response times for frequently accessed data. Which AWS service is best suited for this purpose?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon S3","Amazon ElastiCache","Amazon DynamoDB","Amazon EBS"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is Amazon ElastiCache. ElastiCache is a fully managed in-memory caching service that supports popular open-source caching engines like Redis and Memcached. It is ideal for reducing database load and improving application response times by caching frequently accessed data. Option A (Amazon S3) is object storage. Option C (Amazon DynamoDB) is a NoSQL database service. Option D (Amazon EBS) provides block storage for EC2 instances. None of these are in-memory caching services.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  80,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_3_55',
  'What is the primary benefit of using asynchronous processing for tasks that don''t require an immediate response in a web application?',
  'mcq',
  '["It ensures that all tasks are completed in real-time.","It simplifies the debugging process for complex workflows.","It improves the responsiveness of the user interface and overall application throughput.","It reduces the total cost of computing resources by eliminating idle time."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is the primary benefit of asynchronous processing. By offloading long-running or non-critical tasks to be processed in the background, the main application thread or API can respond immediately to the user, improving user interface responsiveness and allowing the application to handle more requests (higher throughput). Option A is incorrect; asynchronous processing implies tasks might not be real-time. Option B is incorrect; asynchronous workflows can sometimes be more complex to debug due to distributed nature. Option D is incorrect; while it can optimize resource usage, it doesn''t necessarily eliminate idle time or always reduce total cost, but rather shifts it.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  81,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_3_56',
  'A developer is designing a system where an AWS Lambda function processes messages from an Amazon SQS queue. To optimize for cost-effectiveness and reduce the number of Lambda invocations, which SQS feature should be leveraged?',
  'mcq',
  '["SQS FIFO queues","SQS long polling","SQS batch processing with Lambda","SQS message timers"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is SQS batch processing with Lambda. By configuring a Lambda function to process messages in batches from an SQS queue, the number of Lambda invocations can be significantly reduced. Lambda can process up to 10,000 messages in a single batch, which is more cost-effective than invoking the function for each individual message. Option A (SQS FIFO queues) ensures message ordering and exactly-once processing, but doesn''t directly optimize for cost-effective processing volume. Option B (SQS long polling) reduces the number of empty responses when polling, but doesn''t inherently reduce Lambda invocations per message. Option D (SQS message timers) delays message delivery, which is unrelated to batch processing for cost optimization.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  82,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_3_57',
  'An AWS Lambda function experiences high latency during peak traffic due to cold starts. To ensure consistent, low-latency performance for a critical API, which Lambda feature should be enabled?',
  'mcq',
  '["Reserved Concurrency","Provisioned Concurrency","Memory tuning","Asynchronous invocation"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is Provisioned Concurrency. This feature keeps Lambda functions initialized and hyper-ready to respond in milliseconds. It eliminates cold starts for a specified number of concurrent executions, making it ideal for latency-sensitive applications that require consistent performance during peak traffic. Option A (Reserved Concurrency) guarantees a maximum number of concurrent invocations for a function but does not eliminate cold starts. Option C (Memory tuning) can reduce execution duration but doesn''t prevent cold starts. Option D (Asynchronous invocation) changes how the function is invoked but does not address cold start latency.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  83,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_3_58',
  'A developer has an Amazon API Gateway endpoint that serves frequently accessed, static configuration data to mobile clients. The data changes only once a day. To reduce the load on the backend AWS Lambda function and improve response times for clients, which optimization should be implemented?',
  'mcq',
  '["Increase the memory allocation for the AWS Lambda function.","Implement API Gateway caching with an appropriate Time-To-Live (TTL).","Configure AWS WAF to filter requests to the API Gateway.","Migrate the Lambda function to run on Amazon EC2 for better performance."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is to implement API Gateway caching with an appropriate Time-To-Live (TTL). For frequently accessed, rarely changed endpoints, API Gateway caching drastically reduces the number of invocations to the backend Lambda function, thereby reducing cost and latency (as per anti-pattern ap-4.3-001). Option A (increasing Lambda memory) might slightly reduce execution time but won''t prevent repeated invocations for the same static data. Option C (AWS WAF) is for security, not performance optimization for static data. Option D (migrating to EC2) would significantly increase operational overhead and is not a suitable solution for a serverless API serving static data.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  84,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_3_59',
  'An AWS Lambda function processes real-time sensor data and needs to respond with consistent, low latency even during sudden spikes in traffic. The function is occasionally experiencing cold starts, which introduces undesirable delays. Which optimization strategy should the developer apply to ensure predictable low latency?',
  'mcq',
  '["Increase the Lambda function''s timeout setting.","Implement SQS batch processing for the sensor data.","Enable Provisioned Concurrency for the Lambda function.","Switch to asynchronous invocation for the Lambda function."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is to enable Provisioned Concurrency for the Lambda function. Provisioned Concurrency keeps a specified number of execution environments initialized and ready to respond immediately, effectively eliminating cold starts and ensuring consistent, low-latency performance, which is critical for real-time applications. Option A (increasing timeout) addresses functions running too long, not cold start latency. Option B (SQS batch processing) is for cost-effective message processing, not for reducing cold starts or ensuring real-time low latency. Option D (asynchronous invocation) changes the invocation model but does not prevent cold starts for the function itself.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  85,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_3_60',
  'A web application relies heavily on an Amazon DynamoDB table for user profiles. During peak hours, the application experiences high read latency for user profile lookups, impacting user experience. The user profile data is updated infrequently. Which AWS service should the developer integrate to significantly improve read performance for these lookups?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon ElastiCache for Redis","Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL","Amazon DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX)","Amazon Redshift"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is Amazon DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX). DAX is a fully managed, highly available, in-memory cache specifically designed to sit in front of DynamoDB tables. It provides microsecond response times for read-heavy, eventually consistent workloads, significantly improving performance and reducing the load on the DynamoDB table. Option A (Amazon ElastiCache for Redis) is a general-purpose in-memory cache, but DAX is purpose-built and optimized for DynamoDB. Option B (Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL) is a relational database, not a caching solution for DynamoDB. Option D (Amazon Redshift) is a data warehouse, not suitable for improving operational read performance for DynamoDB.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  86,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_3_61',
  'A backend service processes millions of small messages daily, each triggering an AWS Lambda function. The current setup invokes Lambda for every single message, leading to high invocation costs. To process these messages more cost-effectively, which architectural change should be implemented?',
  'mcq',
  '["Increase the memory allocation for the Lambda function.","Configure the Lambda function to process messages in batches from an Amazon SQS queue.","Migrate the message processing logic to an Amazon EC2 instance.","Implement API Gateway caching for the Lambda function."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is to configure the Lambda function to process messages in batches from an Amazon SQS queue. This allows a single Lambda invocation to process multiple messages (up to 10,000 messages per batch), drastically reducing the total number of Lambda invocations and thus lowering costs. Option A (increasing Lambda memory) might reduce duration but won''t reduce the number of invocations. Option C (migrating to EC2) would increase operational overhead and might not be more cost-effective for bursty, event-driven workloads. Option D (API Gateway caching) is irrelevant here as the messages are coming from SQS, not an API Gateway.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  87,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_3',
  'medium'
);

