VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_3_62',
  'To improve the cold start performance of a critical AWS Lambda function that is invoked frequently, a developer should configure which of the following?',
  'mcq',
  '["Reserved Concurrency","Provisioned Concurrency","Memory tuning","Asynchronous invocation"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is Provisioned Concurrency. This feature keeps a specified number of execution environments initialized and ready to respond immediately, effectively eliminating cold starts and ensuring consistent, low-latency performance for frequently invoked functions. Option A (Reserved Concurrency) guarantees a maximum number of concurrent invocations but does not prevent cold starts. Option C (Memory tuning) can reduce execution duration but doesn''t directly address cold starts. Option D (Asynchronous invocation) changes the invocation model but does not prevent cold starts for the function itself.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  88,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_3_63',
  'A developer needs to optimize an AWS Lambda function that processes large image files. The function is currently experiencing ''out of memory'' errors. Which Lambda performance optimization strategy should be applied?',
  'mcq',
  '["Increase the function''s timeout setting.","Increase the memory allocation for the Lambda function.","Enable Provisioned Concurrency.","Configure SQS batch processing."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is to increase the memory allocation for the Lambda function. ''Out of memory'' errors directly indicate that the function does not have enough memory to complete its execution. Increasing the memory allocation provides more resources to the function, resolving this specific error. Option A (increasing timeout) addresses functions running too long, not memory issues. Option C (Provisioned Concurrency) addresses cold starts, not memory. Option D (SQS batch processing) is for cost-effective message processing, not for resolving memory errors within a single function invocation.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  89,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_3_64',
  'A serverless application processes user-uploaded images. The image processing is a CPU-intensive task performed by an AWS Lambda function. Users upload images asynchronously, and the processing time can vary significantly. The development team wants to optimize the Lambda function for performance and cost-effectiveness. They have observed that increasing memory allocation also increases CPU power proportionally. Which optimization strategy should the developer prioritize to achieve the BEST balance of performance and cost?',
  'mcq',
  '["Increase the Lambda function''s timeout to accommodate longer processing times for all images.","Implement asynchronous processing using Amazon SQS to decouple the upload from processing.","Tune the Lambda function''s memory allocation to find the optimal balance between cost and execution duration.","Enable API Gateway caching for the image upload endpoint to reduce redundant processing."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is to tune the Lambda function''s memory allocation. For CPU-intensive tasks, increasing memory also proportionally increases the CPU power available to the Lambda function. By systematically testing different memory allocations, the developer can find a point where the execution duration is significantly reduced, leading to lower overall cost (as billing is duration * memory) and improved performance. This is a core Lambda performance optimization strategy. Option A (incorrect) is increasing the timeout. While it prevents timeouts, it doesn''t optimize performance or cost; it merely allows slow executions to complete, potentially increasing costs for consistently slow functions. Option B (incorrect) is implementing asynchronous processing using SQS. This is a good architectural pattern for decoupling and improving throughput, but it doesn''t directly optimize the *performance or cost* of the individual Lambda function''s CPU-intensive image processing task itself. Option D (incorrect) is enabling API Gateway caching. This is irrelevant for image *upload* processing, as each upload is unique and not cacheable. Caching is for frequently accessed, rarely changed *responses* (anti-pattern ap-4.3-001).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  90,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_3_65',
  'A media company uses AWS Lambda functions to process video files uploaded to Amazon S3. The processing involves several steps, including transcoding, thumbnail generation, and metadata extraction. These tasks are computationally intensive and can take varying amounts of time, from a few seconds to several minutes. The company wants to optimize the Lambda functions to handle these diverse workloads efficiently and cost-effectively, ensuring that resources are scaled appropriately for each task. Which Lambda optimization technique is MOST suitable for this scenario?',
  'mcq',
  '["Set a fixed, high memory allocation for all Lambda functions to ensure fast processing.","Implement a single, large Lambda function to perform all processing steps sequentially.","Utilize AWS Lambda Powertools to optimize logging and tracing for performance analysis.","Decompose the processing into smaller, specialized Lambda functions and optimize memory for each."]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Option D (correct) is to decompose the processing into smaller, specialized Lambda functions and optimize memory for each. This allows for fine-grained control over resource allocation. For example, a thumbnail generation function might need less memory/CPU than a video transcoding function. By breaking down the workload and tuning memory (and thus CPU) for each specific task, the company can achieve significant cost savings and performance improvements, as Lambda billing is based on duration and memory. Option A (incorrect) is setting a fixed, high memory allocation for all functions. This is inefficient and costly, as many tasks might not require high resources, leading to over-provisioning and higher bills. Option B (incorrect) is implementing a single, large Lambda function. This creates a monolithic function that is harder to optimize, debug, and scale. If one step fails, the entire function might need to be retried, and resource allocation would be a compromise for all steps, not optimized for each. Option C (incorrect) is utilizing AWS Lambda Powertools. While Powertools is excellent for observability (logging, tracing, metrics), it is an instrumentation tool, not a direct performance optimization technique for resource allocation or workload decomposition.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  91,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_3_66',
  'A financial services application uses AWS Lambda functions to process transaction requests. Each request involves multiple steps, including validation, database updates, and external API calls. The application experiences occasional spikes in traffic, leading to increased latency and potential throttling of downstream services. The transactions are critical and must be processed reliably, but they can tolerate a few seconds of delay. The development team needs to implement a solution that improves resilience and throughput during peak loads while minimizing the risk of data loss. Which architectural pattern should they implement?',
  'mcq',
  '["Increase the provisioned concurrency for the AWS Lambda functions to handle peak traffic.","Implement API Gateway caching for the transaction processing endpoint to reduce Lambda invocations.","Introduce an Amazon SQS queue to decouple the request ingestion from the Lambda processing.","Migrate the entire application to Amazon EC2 instances with Auto Scaling to handle variable load."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is to introduce an Amazon SQS queue to decouple the request ingestion from the Lambda processing. Since transactions can tolerate a few seconds of delay and reliability is paramount, SQS acts as a robust buffer. Incoming requests are placed in the queue, and Lambda functions process them asynchronously. This decouples the producer (request ingestion) from the consumer (Lambda processing), absorbs traffic spikes, prevents Lambda throttling, and ensures that all messages are eventually processed, even if downstream services are temporarily overloaded. This significantly improves resilience and throughput. Option A (incorrect) is increasing provisioned concurrency. While this helps with Lambda cold starts and consistent performance, it''s a scaling mechanism for Lambda itself and doesn''t provide the buffering or decoupling benefits of SQS for protecting downstream services or handling sustained, unpredictable spikes. Option B (incorrect) is implementing API Gateway caching. Caching is suitable for frequently accessed, rarely changing data (anti-pattern ap-4.3-001). Transaction requests are unique and dynamic, making caching inappropriate for this use case. Option D (incorrect) is migrating to Amazon EC2. This would introduce significant operational overhead for managing servers, which goes against the serverless nature of the current application and is a much more complex solution than simply adding an SQS queue for buffering.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  92,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_3_67',
  'A backend service processes user-submitted forms using an AWS Lambda function. The forms are submitted via an Amazon API Gateway endpoint. The Lambda function performs data validation and then stores the data in an Amazon DynamoDB table. During peak periods, the Lambda function experiences throttling, and some form submissions fail. The form submissions are critical but can tolerate a delay of up to 30 seconds. The development team wants to ensure all submissions are processed reliably without increasing the Lambda concurrency limit or incurring high costs. Which architectural change should they implement?',
  'mcq',
  '["Increase the memory allocation for the Lambda function to improve processing speed.","Implement API Gateway caching for the form submission endpoint to reduce Lambda invocations.","Configure the API Gateway to integrate with an Amazon SQS queue for asynchronous processing.","Set up AWS X-Ray tracing to identify bottlenecks in the Lambda function''s execution."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is to configure the API Gateway to integrate with an Amazon SQS queue for asynchronous processing. Since form submissions are critical but can tolerate a delay, using an SQS queue decouples the API Gateway from the Lambda function. API Gateway can directly send messages to SQS, which then buffers the messages. The Lambda function can process messages from the SQS queue at its own pace, even during peak load, ensuring all submissions are eventually processed reliably without overwhelming the Lambda function or incurring high costs from increased concurrency limits. Option A (incorrect) is increasing Lambda memory. While this can speed up processing, it doesn''t solve the issue of throttling if the invocation rate exceeds the concurrency limit, nor does it provide buffering for peak loads. Option B (incorrect) is implementing API Gateway caching. Form submissions are unique data, so caching is inappropriate and would not reduce the number of actual submissions or the load on the backend. This is an anti-pattern (ap-4.3-001). Option D (incorrect) is setting up AWS X-Ray tracing. X-Ray is a monitoring and debugging tool, not a solution for handling peak load or ensuring reliable processing during throttling. It helps identify *why* throttling occurs but doesn''t prevent it.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  93,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_3_68',
  'A company runs a data ingestion pipeline where millions of small data records are sent to an Amazon SQS queue. An AWS Lambda function is configured to process these messages. Currently, the Lambda function processes each SQS message individually, leading to a very high number of Lambda invocations and associated costs. The records can be processed in batches without issues. The development team wants to reduce the number of Lambda invocations and optimize costs while maintaining high throughput. Which SQS-Lambda integration feature should they configure?',
  'mcq',
  '["Increase the Lambda function''s memory allocation to process messages faster.","Configure the SQS queue with a longer message retention period.","Enable SQS batch processing for the Lambda event source mapping with a suitable batch size.","Implement a custom retry mechanism within the Lambda function for failed SQS message processing."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is to enable SQS batch processing for the Lambda event source mapping with a suitable batch size. AWS Lambda allows configuring an SQS event source to send messages to the Lambda function in batches (up to 10,000 messages per batch). This significantly reduces the number of Lambda invocations, which directly translates to lower costs and improved throughput, as the Lambda function processes multiple messages in a single execution. Option A (incorrect) is increasing Lambda memory. While this might speed up the processing of *each batch*, it doesn''t reduce the *number* of invocations if messages are still processed individually. The core issue is the invocation model, not just the speed of a single message. Option B (incorrect) is configuring a longer SQS message retention period. This affects how long messages are stored in the queue but has no impact on how Lambda processes them or the number of invocations. Option D (incorrect) is implementing a custom retry mechanism. This is for handling failures, not for optimizing the cost or throughput of successful batch processing.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  94,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_3_69',
  'A serverless application uses an AWS Lambda function to generate reports. The report generation process is CPU-intensive and can take up to 2 minutes. The Lambda function is configured with 128 MB of memory. Users are complaining about slow report generation. The development team observes that increasing the memory allocation for Lambda functions also proportionally increases the available CPU power. To reduce the report generation time and potentially optimize costs, which action should the developer take?',
  'mcq',
  '["Increase the Lambda function''s timeout to 3 minutes to prevent premature termination.","Implement Amazon CloudFront caching for the generated reports.","Gradually increase the Lambda function''s memory allocation and monitor its duration and cost.","Migrate the report generation logic to an Amazon EC2 instance for more dedicated CPU resources."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is to gradually increase the Lambda function''s memory allocation and monitor its duration and cost. For CPU-intensive tasks, increasing memory also increases the available CPU power. By systematically testing higher memory settings, the developer can find an optimal point where the execution duration is significantly reduced, potentially leading to lower overall costs (as billing is duration * memory) and faster report generation. Tools like AWS Lambda Power Tuning can automate this. Option A (incorrect) is increasing the timeout. While it prevents timeouts, it doesn''t optimize performance or cost; it merely allows slow executions to complete, potentially increasing costs for consistently slow functions. Option B (incorrect) is implementing CloudFront caching. Caching is for serving static or pre-generated content faster, not for optimizing the *generation process* itself. Option D (incorrect) is migrating to Amazon EC2. This would introduce significant operational overhead for managing servers, which goes against the serverless nature of the current application and is a much more complex solution than optimizing the existing Lambda function.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  95,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_3',
  'hard'
);

