INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_2_76',
  'A development team is building a new application on Amazon EC2 instances and needs to send custom application-specific metrics to Amazon CloudWatch. Which methods can they use to effectively emit these custom metrics? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Using the PutMetricData API","Using the CloudWatch Agent","Sending metrics via Amazon SQS","Logging metrics to Amazon S3","Using AWS Config rules"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Using the PutMetricData API) allows applications to directly send custom metric data points to Amazon CloudWatch. Option B (Using the CloudWatch Agent) is a recommended way to collect system-level metrics, application logs, and custom metrics from EC2 instances and on-premises servers, consolidating the collection process. Option C (Sending metrics via Amazon SQS) is not a direct or recommended method for sending metrics to CloudWatch; SQS is a messaging service. Option D (Logging metrics to Amazon S3) stores log files but doesn''t automatically ingest them as CloudWatch metrics. Option E (Using AWS Config rules) is for evaluating resource configurations, not for emitting custom application metrics.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  40,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_2_77',
  'What are the primary advantages of using CloudWatch Embedded Metrics Format (EMF) for emitting custom metrics from serverless applications? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Combining high-cardinality logs and metrics in a single log event","Automatic extraction of metrics from structured log events","Reduced cold start times for AWS Lambda functions","Direct integration with Amazon DynamoDB tables","Simplified deployment of containerized applications"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Combining high-cardinality logs and metrics in a single log event) is a key benefit of EMF, allowing developers to include detailed context (logs) alongside metric data, which is especially useful for high-cardinality data. Option B (Automatic extraction of metrics from structured log events) is another core advantage, as CloudWatch automatically parses the EMF-formatted logs and extracts the embedded metrics, simplifying the metric collection process. Option C (Reduced cold start times for AWS Lambda functions) is unrelated to EMF. Option D (Direct integration with Amazon DynamoDB tables) is not a feature of EMF. Option E (Simplified deployment of containerized applications) is also unrelated to EMF.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  41,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_2_78',
  'A developer is building a serverless application that processes user requests through Amazon API Gateway, an AWS Lambda function, and stores data in Amazon DynamoDB. To gain end-to-end visibility and trace requests across these services, which components should be instrumented with the AWS X-Ray SDK? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["The AWS Lambda function code","The Amazon DynamoDB client calls within the Lambda function","The Amazon API Gateway stage configuration","The Amazon CloudWatch Logs configuration","The AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) roles"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (The AWS Lambda function code) should be instrumented with the X-Ray SDK to capture segments for the Lambda function''s execution. Option B (The Amazon DynamoDB client calls within the Lambda function) should also be instrumented (often automatically by the SDK when wrapping the AWS SDK client) to trace calls to DynamoDB as subsegments, providing visibility into database interactions. Option C (The Amazon API Gateway stage configuration) can enable X-Ray tracing for API Gateway itself, but the question asks about SDK instrumentation, which applies to code. Option D (The Amazon CloudWatch Logs configuration) is for logging, not tracing. Option E (The AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) roles) define permissions, not instrumentation.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  42,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_2_79',
  'A development team wants to track specific business-level metrics (e.g., successful payment count, user signup rate) from their serverless application, which uses AWS Lambda functions. Which methods can they use to emit these custom metrics efficiently to Amazon CloudWatch? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Using CloudWatch Embedded Metrics Format (EMF)","Making direct PutMetricData API calls","Writing metrics to Amazon S3 and importing them","Using Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose to stream metrics","Configuring AWS Config rules"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Using CloudWatch Embedded Metrics Format (EMF)) allows developers to emit custom metrics by embedding them within structured log events, which CloudWatch automatically extracts. This is efficient for high-volume and high-cardinality metrics. Option B (Making direct PutMetricData API calls) is the fundamental way to send custom metrics to CloudWatch from any application or service. Option C (Writing metrics to Amazon S3 and importing them) is an indirect and less efficient method for real-time metric ingestion. Option D (Using Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose to stream metrics) is a data delivery service, not a direct method for emitting custom metrics to CloudWatch. Option E (Configuring AWS Config rules) is for compliance and configuration management, not for emitting application metrics.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  43,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_2_80',
  'Which of the following are key benefits of using AWS Lambda Powertools for Python in serverless applications? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Simplified structured logging and log correlation","Automated X-Ray tracing and segment management","Reduced AWS account billing for Lambda invocations","Automatic generation of API Gateway endpoints","Enhanced security posture for IAM roles"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Simplified structured logging and log correlation) is a core benefit, as Powertools provides decorators and utilities to easily implement structured logging, which improves log analysis and correlation. Option B (Automated X-Ray tracing and segment management) is another key feature, as Powertools integrates with the X-Ray SDK to automatically capture traces and manage segments, reducing manual instrumentation effort. Option C (Reduced AWS account billing for Lambda invocations) is not a direct benefit of Powertools; it''s an observability tool, not a cost-optimization feature. Option D (Automatic generation of API Gateway endpoints) is a function of frameworks like AWS SAM or Serverless Framework, not Powertools. Option E (Enhanced security posture for IAM roles) is related to security best practices, not a direct feature of Powertools.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  44,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_2_81',
  'A developer is tasked with improving the observability of a new serverless application that uses AWS Lambda functions, Amazon DynamoDB, and Amazon S3. The application processes user-uploaded images, and the team wants to capture detailed performance metrics, including custom business-specific metrics like `ImageProcessingTime` and `ImageSize`, without incurring high costs for high-cardinality data. Which two approaches should the developer implement to achieve this? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Use CloudWatch Embedded Metrics Format (EMF) within the Lambda functions to emit custom metrics.","Integrate AWS X-Ray SDK into the Lambda functions to trace image processing workflows.","Add `ImageSize` and `UserID` as custom dimensions to standard CloudWatch metrics.","Store detailed processing logs in Amazon S3 and analyze them with Amazon Athena.","Create custom CloudWatch Alarms based on the `ImageProcessingTime` metric."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is the recommended approach for emitting custom metrics, especially with high-cardinality dimensions, without incurring high costs. CloudWatch Embedded Metrics Format (EMF) allows developers to embed metric data into structured log events, which are then automatically extracted and published as CloudWatch metrics. This is cost-effective for high-cardinality data like `ImageSize` and `UserID` because the detailed dimensions are part of the log, not separate metric dimensions. Option B (correct) is crucial for understanding the end-to-end flow and performance. Integrating AWS X-Ray SDK into the Lambda functions will provide distributed tracing, allowing the team to visualize the entire image processing workflow, including calls to DynamoDB and S3, and identify performance bottlenecks within the process. Option C (incorrect) is an anti-pattern for high-cardinality data. Adding `ImageSize` and `UserID` as custom dimensions to standard CloudWatch metrics would create a unique metric for every distinct combination of these values, leading to extremely high costs and potentially exceeding CloudWatch limits. Option D (incorrect) is a valid approach for long-term log analysis but is not the most direct or real-time method for emitting and monitoring custom performance metrics. Option E (incorrect) is a subsequent step after metrics are emitted; it doesn''t describe how to *implement* the capture of custom metrics.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  45,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_2_82',
  'A development team is building a new microservices application using AWS Lambda and Amazon API Gateway. They want to ensure comprehensive observability for their application, including distributed tracing, structured logging, and custom business metrics, while minimizing the operational overhead of instrumenting their code. Which two AWS services or tools should they prioritize for integration into their Lambda functions? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Lambda Powertools for Python/Java/TypeScript.","AWS X-Ray SDK for distributed tracing.","Amazon CloudWatch Logs Insights for log analysis.","AWS Config for resource configuration tracking.","Amazon GuardDuty for threat detection."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is highly recommended for serverless applications. AWS Lambda Powertools provides a set of utilities for Lambda functions to implement structured logging, custom metrics (using EMF), and distributed tracing (integrating with X-Ray) with minimal code changes, significantly reducing operational overhead and promoting best practices. Option B (correct) is fundamental for distributed tracing. The AWS X-Ray SDK enables developers to instrument their Lambda functions to send trace data to AWS X-Ray, providing an end-to-end view of requests across multiple services, which is crucial for debugging and performance analysis in microservices. Option C (incorrect) is a tool for *analyzing* logs, not for *instrumenting* code to generate logs or metrics. While essential for observability, it''s not an instrumentation tool. Option D (incorrect) is for tracking resource configuration changes and compliance, not for application-level observability. Option E (incorrect) is a threat detection service, unrelated to application instrumentation for performance and error monitoring.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  46,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_2_83',
  'A developer is tasked with instrumenting an existing AWS Lambda function that processes customer orders. The team needs to capture several custom business metrics, such as `OrderValue` and `PaymentMethod`, and wants to include `CustomerID` for detailed analysis. However, they are concerned about the cost implications of using high-cardinality dimensions with standard Amazon CloudWatch metrics. Which two strategies should the developer employ to achieve this observability requirement cost-effectively? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Use CloudWatch Embedded Metrics Format (EMF) to embed `OrderValue`, `PaymentMethod`, and `CustomerID` within structured log events.","Store `CustomerID` and other high-cardinality data as custom attributes within AWS X-Ray segments.","Create separate CloudWatch metrics for `OrderValue` and `PaymentMethod` with `CustomerID` as a dimension.","Log `CustomerID` as part of structured log messages in Amazon CloudWatch Logs and use CloudWatch Logs Insights for analysis.","Utilize Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose to stream all custom metrics to an external analytics platform."]'::json,
  '{0,3}',
  'Option A (correct) is the most cost-effective way to handle high-cardinality metrics. CloudWatch Embedded Metrics Format (EMF) allows embedding detailed metric data, including high-cardinality dimensions like `CustomerID`, directly into structured log events. CloudWatch then automatically extracts and publishes these as metrics, while the detailed dimensions remain in the logs for granular analysis via CloudWatch Logs Insights, avoiding the high cost of unique metric dimension combinations. Option D (correct) is also a highly cost-effective and recommended approach for high-cardinality data. Instead of using `CustomerID` as a metric dimension, logging it as part of structured log messages in Amazon CloudWatch Logs allows for powerful and flexible analysis using CloudWatch Logs Insights queries without incurring the per-metric cost of high-cardinality dimensions. Option B (incorrect) is suitable for tracing and debugging, but X-Ray custom attributes are not designed for aggregate metric analysis or cost-effective storage of high-cardinality data for general business metrics. Option C (incorrect) represents an anti-pattern. Creating CloudWatch metrics with `CustomerID` as a dimension would generate a distinct metric for every unique customer, leading to extremely high costs and potential service limits for high-cardinality data. Option E (incorrect) involves streaming data to an external platform, which adds complexity and cost, and doesn''t directly address the requirement of capturing custom metrics within AWS cost-effectively.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  47,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_2_84',
  'A new serverless application processes millions of user requests daily through Amazon API Gateway and AWS Lambda. The development team needs to instrument the application to capture end-to-end request tracing, detailed structured logs for debugging, and custom metrics for business insights. They want to achieve this with minimal code changes and adhere to AWS best practices for observability. Which two AWS services or tools should be integrated into the Lambda functions? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Lambda Powertools for structured logging, tracing, and metrics.","AWS X-Ray SDK for distributed tracing.","Amazon CloudWatch Logs Insights for log analysis.","Amazon EventBridge for event-driven architectures.","AWS Step Functions for orchestrating workflows."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is an excellent choice for serverless applications. AWS Lambda Powertools provides a suite of utilities that simplify the implementation of structured logging, custom metrics (using Embedded Metrics Format), and distributed tracing (integrating with X-Ray) within Lambda functions, significantly reducing boilerplate code and promoting best practices. Option B (correct) is fundamental for end-to-end request tracing. Integrating the AWS X-Ray SDK into Lambda functions allows the application to send trace data to X-Ray, providing a visual service map and detailed timelines of requests as they flow through API Gateway, Lambda, and other downstream services, which is crucial for debugging and performance analysis. Option C (incorrect) is a powerful tool for *querying* and *analyzing* logs once they are in CloudWatch Logs, but it is not an instrumentation tool that you integrate into your code to *generate* logs or traces. Option D (incorrect) is an event bus service for building event-driven architectures, not an observability tool for instrumenting code. Option E (incorrect) is a serverless workflow orchestrator, not an instrumentation tool for capturing logs, traces, or metrics.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  48,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_2_85',
  'A developer is working on a critical AWS Lambda function that performs complex data transformations. The team needs to monitor the function''s execution to identify potential performance bottlenecks and memory leaks. They also want to capture custom metrics about the transformation process, such as the number of records processed per invocation, without impacting the function''s latency significantly. Which two instrumentation strategies should the developer implement? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Enable AWS X-Ray active tracing for the Lambda function.","Emit custom metrics using CloudWatch Embedded Metrics Format (EMF).","Log all transformation details to Amazon S3 for later analysis.","Configure a high-resolution custom metric in CloudWatch for each record processed.","Use Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose to stream logs and metrics to a third-party tool."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is essential for performance analysis. Enabling AWS X-Ray active tracing provides detailed visibility into the Lambda function''s execution, including subsegments for different parts of the code and external calls. This helps identify bottlenecks, understand latency distribution, and pinpoint areas that might be contributing to memory issues or slow performance. Option B (correct) is an efficient way to capture custom metrics. CloudWatch Embedded Metrics Format (EMF) allows developers to emit multiple custom metrics from a single log event, which are then automatically extracted by CloudWatch. This approach is highly performant as it only requires writing a log line, and it''s cost-effective for capturing various metrics like ''records processed per invocation''. Option C (incorrect) is suitable for archival and batch analysis but is not a real-time monitoring solution for performance bottlenecks or a direct way to emit custom metrics for immediate visibility. Option D (incorrect) is an anti-pattern for high-volume, granular metrics. Creating a high-resolution custom metric for *each record processed* would lead to an explosion of metrics and significantly increase CloudWatch costs, as well as potentially hitting service limits. EMF is a better solution for this. Option E (incorrect) involves streaming data to an external tool, which adds complexity and cost, and is not the primary or most direct way to instrument the Lambda function for native AWS monitoring.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  49,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_2_86',
  'A company is developing a new serverless application that processes sensitive financial transactions. The application uses AWS Lambda functions and Amazon DynamoDB. The development team needs to ensure that all application logs contain structured data for easier querying and analysis, and that critical business events are captured as custom metrics. They also want to trace requests end-to-end across services. Which two best practices should they implement in their Lambda functions? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Implement structured logging using a library like AWS Lambda Powertools.","Integrate the AWS X-Ray SDK for distributed tracing.","Use Amazon CloudWatch Logs Insights for real-time log streaming.","Configure Amazon SQS dead-letter queues for all Lambda functions.","Set up Amazon EventBridge rules for cross-service communication."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is a best practice for modern application development. Implementing structured logging, ideally with a library like AWS Lambda Powertools, ensures that logs are in a consistent, machine-readable format (e.g., JSON). This makes it significantly easier to query, filter, and analyze log data in Amazon CloudWatch Logs Insights for debugging and operational insights. Option B (correct) is crucial for understanding the flow and performance of requests in a distributed system. Integrating the AWS X-Ray SDK enables end-to-end distributed tracing, providing a visual service map and detailed timelines of requests as they traverse through Lambda, DynamoDB, and other services, which is essential for identifying bottlenecks and errors. Option C (incorrect) is a tool for *analyzing* logs, not for *implementing* structured logging or tracing within the Lambda function code itself. Option D (incorrect) is a fault-tolerance mechanism for handling failed messages, not an observability practice for instrumenting code. Option E (incorrect) is for building event-driven architectures and routing events, not for instrumenting individual Lambda functions for observability.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  50,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_2_87',
  'A developer is building a high-throughput data ingestion pipeline using AWS Lambda functions. The team needs to capture detailed metrics about the incoming data, such as `PayloadSize` and `RecordCount`, and also include a `BatchID` for each invocation. They want to avoid creating an excessive number of custom CloudWatch metrics due to cost concerns, especially given the high variability of `BatchID`. Which two strategies are most appropriate for instrumenting the Lambda function to meet these requirements cost-effectively? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Utilize CloudWatch Embedded Metrics Format (EMF) to publish `PayloadSize` and `RecordCount` metrics, embedding `BatchID` in the log event.","Log `BatchID` and other high-cardinality data as structured log events to Amazon CloudWatch Logs, and use CloudWatch Logs Insights for analysis.","Create a custom CloudWatch metric for `PayloadSize` and `RecordCount`, with `BatchID` as a high-cardinality dimension.","Send all detailed metrics and logs to an Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose delivery stream for processing by an external analytics tool.","Add `BatchID` as an annotation to AWS X-Ray segments for each Lambda invocation."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is an ideal solution for publishing multiple metrics efficiently, especially when dealing with high-cardinality data. CloudWatch Embedded Metrics Format (EMF) allows developers to embed metric data within structured log events. CloudWatch automatically extracts the metrics (`PayloadSize`, `RecordCount`) while the `BatchID` remains in the log, making it available for detailed analysis via CloudWatch Logs Insights without incurring the cost of high-cardinality metric dimensions. Option B (correct) is a robust and cost-effective strategy for handling high-cardinality data. By logging `BatchID` and other detailed information as structured log events to Amazon CloudWatch Logs, developers can leverage CloudWatch Logs Insights to perform powerful queries and analysis without creating expensive custom metrics with high-cardinality dimensions. Option C (incorrect) is an anti-pattern. Using `BatchID` as a dimension for a custom CloudWatch metric would create a new metric series for every unique `BatchID`, leading to prohibitive costs and potential service limits due to the high cardinality. Option D (incorrect) involves sending data outside of CloudWatch, which adds complexity, potential latency, and additional costs for the Firehose stream and the external tool, when the requirements can be met natively within CloudWatch more cost-effectively. Option E (incorrect) is suitable for tracing and debugging individual requests, but X-Ray annotations are not designed for aggregate metric analysis or cost-effective storage of high-cardinality data for general business metrics.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  51,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_2_88',
  'A development team is building a new serverless application that processes financial transactions. The application uses AWS Lambda functions and Amazon DynamoDB. They need to ensure that all application logs are structured for easy querying, and that critical business events (e.g., `TransactionSuccess`, `TransactionFailure`) are captured as custom metrics. They also want to trace requests end-to-end across services. Which two best practices should they implement in their Lambda functions to achieve comprehensive observability with minimal operational overhead? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Integrate AWS Lambda Powertools for structured logging, tracing, and metrics.","Enable AWS X-Ray active tracing for all Lambda functions.","Use Amazon CloudWatch Logs Insights for interactive log analysis.","Configure Amazon SQS dead-letter queues for failed transactions.","Set up Amazon EventBridge rules for transaction status notifications."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is highly recommended for serverless applications. AWS Lambda Powertools provides a set of utilities that simplify the implementation of structured logging, custom metrics (using Embedded Metrics Format, EMF), and distributed tracing (integrating with X-Ray) within Lambda functions. This significantly reduces boilerplate code and operational overhead while promoting best practices for observability. Option B (correct) is fundamental for understanding the end-to-end flow of requests in a distributed system. Enabling AWS X-Ray active tracing for Lambda functions provides a visual service map and detailed timelines of requests as they traverse through Lambda, DynamoDB, and other services, which is crucial for identifying bottlenecks, errors, and understanding transaction paths. Option C (incorrect) is a powerful tool for *analyzing* logs once they are in CloudWatch Logs, but it is not an instrumentation tool that you integrate into your code to *generate* logs, traces, or metrics. Option D (incorrect) is a fault-tolerance mechanism for handling failed messages or transactions, not an observability practice for instrumenting code. Option E (incorrect) is an event bus service for building event-driven architectures and routing events, not an instrumentation tool for capturing logs, traces, or metrics.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  52,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_31_m_31_2_89',
  'A developer is building a new microservice using AWS Lambda and wants to ensure comprehensive observability for debugging and performance monitoring. Which three methods are effective for instrumenting the application code to achieve this? (Select THREE.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Integrating the AWS X-Ray SDK to capture distributed traces.","Utilizing AWS Lambda Powertools for structured logging, tracing, and metrics.","Emitting custom CloudWatch metrics with appropriate dimensions for key business KPIs.","Configuring API Gateway caching for frequently accessed endpoints.","Increasing the Lambda function''s memory allocation to improve performance."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2}',
  'Option A (correct) allows the application to send trace data to AWS X-Ray, providing a detailed view of requests as they travel through various services, which is essential for distributed system observability. Option B (correct) provides a set of utilities for AWS Lambda functions, including structured logging, custom metrics, and tracing, which simplifies the instrumentation process and promotes best practices. Option C (correct) enables developers to define and publish application-specific metrics to Amazon CloudWatch, offering insights into custom business logic and operational health beyond standard Lambda metrics. Option D (incorrect) is an optimization strategy to reduce backend calls and latency by caching responses at the API Gateway level. It is not a method for instrumenting application code for observability. Option E (incorrect) is a performance optimization technique that can reduce execution duration and cold starts for AWS Lambda functions. It does not directly instrument the code for observability, though it affects performance which might be monitored.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain31_6',
  NOW(),
  53,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_31_2',
  'medium'
);

